window.HELP_INDEX = [{"url":"index.html","page":"PraiseAV Help","heading":"","text":"PraiseAV Help Welcome. PraiseAV puts songs, Bible readings, videos and sermons on your church’s screens. It’s free, and it’s made to be simple enough for any volunteer on a Sunday morning.","summary":"Start here: guides for your first Sunday, every feature, example setups and hardware."},{"url":"index.html#new-to-praiseav","page":"PraiseAV Help","heading":"New to PraiseAV?","text":"New to PraiseAV? Start with Your first Sunday . It takes you from installing PraiseAV to running a whole service, one step at a time. No experience needed. Used EasyWorship before? Coming from EasyWorship shows where everything is, and how to bring your songs and backgrounds across. Songs from OpenLP and ProPresenter come across too: see Coming from OpenLP or ProPresenter .","summary":""},{"url":"index.html#find-what-you-need","page":"PraiseAV Help","heading":"Find what you need","text":"Find what you need Type in the search box at the top of any page: try “projector”, “alert” or “NIV”. Or pick a topic: I want to… Read Add songs and change their words Songs Show a Bible reading, including the NIV The Bible Build a service and save it to take to church Services Print a service sheet for the congregation Printing Run the service on Sunday Running a service Check screens, sound and media before Sunday Ready for Sunday? Give the preacher their notes and a timer Sermons and the preacher’s screen Call a parent from the crèche Congregation alerts Use moving backgrounds, videos and music Media and Music and audio Choose a theme or make seasonal backgrounds Themes and Seasonal backgrounds Show notices before the service Announcement loops Show a guest speaker’s PowerPoint PowerPoint and PDF Make my own presentation slides Slide designer Set up the projector and other screens Screens Livestream the service to YouTube Going live on YouTube Livestream with an ATEM or OBS Livestream and ATEM Record the service to upload later Recording the service Use old phones as cameras Live video Show live English captions Live captions Show PraiseAV to another church The demo church Work PraiseAV from my phone Phone remote Use a MIDI pedal or Stream Deck MIDI and Stream Deck Show words in OBS or another room Screens on the network Let people follow words on their phones Words on phones Manage church settings and backups Church setup and Backups Prepare a song-usage report CCLI report Run a service with no one at the laptop Without an AV volunteer See what equipment other churches use Example setups and Hardware Fix something that isn’t working Troubleshooting Tip: In PraiseAV, click ? Help at the top right, or press F1 at any time to open the help about what you’re doing.","summary":""},{"url":"first-sunday.html","page":"Your first Sunday","heading":"","text":"Your first Sunday This guide takes you from nothing to a whole service on the screen. Take it slowly; you can’t break anything. Allow about half an hour the first time, ideally a day or two before Sunday. You’ll need: a Windows computer or laptop (Windows 10 or 11) your projector or TV, and the cable to connect it (usually HDMI ) if your church used EasyWorship before, the computer it was on","summary":"The beginner's guide: install PraiseAV, plug in the projector, build a service and run it, step by step."},{"url":"first-sunday.html#step-1-install-praiseav","page":"Your first Sunday","heading":"Step 1: Install PraiseAV","text":"Step 1: Install PraiseAV Install PraiseAV from the Microsoft Store (search for “PraiseAV”), or from praiseav.com . Open it from the Start menu. The first time it opens, PraiseAV says hello and asks a few questions. You can come back to them any time with Getting started… at the bottom right of the window. Used EasyWorship? Choose Yes , and PraiseAV brings your songs and backgrounds across. EasyWorship itself isn’t changed. Starting fresh? Choose No . PraiseAV comes with some well-known hymns to start you off. Then type your church’s name and choose your logo (a picture file). The logo is what the screen shows when you press Logo .","summary":""},{"url":"first-sunday.html#step-2-plug-in-the-projector","page":"Your first Sunday","heading":"Step 2: Plug in the projector","text":"Step 2: Plug in the projector Connect the projector (or TV) to the computer with its HDMI cable, and switch it on. Press the Windows key and P together, and choose Extend . PraiseAV puts the words on the projector by itself. Important: It must be Extend , not Duplicate . With Duplicate , the projector just copies your screen, and the congregation would see everything you do. Check: the top of PraiseAV shows a green dot and Congregation (in a narrower window just the green dot: point at it to see the screens). That means the projector is ready. If you’re unsure which screen is which, open Screens… and click Identify screens : each screen shows a big number. See Screens for more.","summary":""},{"url":"first-sunday.html#step-3-find-your-way-around","page":"Your first Sunday","heading":"Step 3: Find your way around","text":"Step 3: Find your way around The window has four columns: Library (left): your songs, the Bible, media (pictures, videos, music) and themes. Service (second): the order of today’s service, top to bottom. Slides (middle): the slides of whatever you’ve picked in the service. On screen (right): exactly what the congregation can see now, and what comes next.","summary":""},{"url":"first-sunday.html#step-4-build-the-service","page":"Your first Sunday","heading":"Step 4: Build the service","text":"Step 4: Build the service Add songs. In the Songs tab, type a few words of the title or the words into the search box, then double-click the song. It goes into the service. Add a Bible reading. Open the Bible tab, type the reference (short forms are fine, like ps 23 or jn 3 16-18 ), then press Enter . Add the sermon. Click + Sermon at the top of the Service column. Type the title and the preacher’s name. If they’ve sent notes, paste them in; they’ll appear only on the preacher’s screen. See Sermons . Change the order. Drag items up and down the service. To take one out, point at it and click the ✕ . Give it a name and save it. Type a name at the top of the Service column, like “Sunday 18 October”, and click Save . See Services to keep services on OneDrive or a USB stick.","summary":""},{"url":"first-sunday.html#step-5-practise","page":"Your first Sunday","heading":"Step 5: Practise","text":"Step 5: Practise Before Sunday, run through the service once, even on your own at home: Click the first item in the service, then click its first slide. It appears on the screen, and the slide has a red border in PraiseAV. Press Next (or the → key) to move on. At the end of a song, Next carries on into the next item. Try Black , Clear text and Logo , and press them again to come back. No projector at home? Open Screens… and click Open practice screens to see what every screen would show. See Screens .","summary":""},{"url":"first-sunday.html#step-6-on-sunday","page":"Your first Sunday","heading":"Step 6: On Sunday","text":"Step 6: On Sunday Switch on the projector and computer early, and open PraiseAV. It remembers the service you were working on. Click ✓ Ready for Sunday? at the top of the Service column. It checks the screens, the videos and pictures, the sound and the laptop, and says what to put right. See Running a service . Before the service, show your Logo (press L ) or your notices . When the first song starts, click its first slide, then press Next at the end of each verse. Watch Up next on the right. It shows exactly what Next will put on the screen, so there are no surprises. Between items, press Logo or Black . Tip: A presentation clicker (the kind used for PowerPoint) works with PraiseAV straight away: forward is Next, back is Previous, and the blank button is Black.","summary":""},{"url":"first-sunday.html#if-something-goes-wrong","page":"Your first Sunday","heading":"If something goes wrong","text":"If something goes wrong The words aren’t on the projector: if there’s a yellow message at the top, click Use it as a separate screen . Otherwise check Screens… . Wrong slide on screen: click the right one; it changes straight away. Need a moment: press Black . Nothing is lost. PraiseAV closed, or the laptop restarted: open PraiseAV again and click Carry on where I was . Everything comes back as it was. More in Troubleshooting . In PraiseAV, click ? Help at the top right (or press F1 ) to come back to this guide at any time. Well done: you’re ready for Sunday.","summary":""},{"url":"coming-from-easyworship.html","page":"Coming from EasyWorship","heading":"","text":"Coming from EasyWorship PraiseAV works the way EasyWorship users expect, so most things will feel familiar. This page shows how to bring your things across and where to find everything.","summary":"Bring your EasyWorship songs, schedules and backgrounds across (or your songs from OpenLP and ProPresenter), and find where everything is in PraiseAV."},{"url":"coming-from-easyworship.html#bring-your-songs-and-backgrounds-across","page":"Coming from EasyWorship","heading":"Bring your songs and backgrounds across","text":"Bring your songs and backgrounds across Do this on the computer that has EasyWorship on it. In PraiseAV, click Getting started… (bottom right), then Yes: bring my songs and backgrounds across . PraiseAV finds EasyWorship and shows how many songs and backgrounds it found. Click Bring in the songs , then Bring them in for the backgrounds, pictures and videos. Songs you already have are skipped, so it’s safe to do again. Backgrounds go into the Media tab, under Backgrounds › From EasyWorship . EasyWorship itself isn’t changed or removed. You can keep using it while you try PraiseAV. Videos in old formats (WMV, AVI, MPEG) can’t be played. The free HandBrake app turns them into MP4, which works. You can also bring songs in from the Songs tab at any time: click + Add songs , then From EasyWorship .","summary":""},{"url":"coming-from-easyworship.html#easyworship-on-a-different-computer","page":"Coming from EasyWorship","heading":"EasyWorship on a different computer","text":"EasyWorship on a different computer Copy EasyWorship’s song library (the files Songs.db and SongWords.db , usually in Public Documents › Softouch › EasyWorship › Default › Databases › Data ) onto a USB stick. Then, in PraiseAV, use + Add songs › From EasyWorship and choose Songs.db from the stick.","summary":""},{"url":"coming-from-easyworship.html#easyworship-schedules","page":"Coming from EasyWorship","heading":"EasyWorship schedules","text":"EasyWorship schedules Saved schedules ( .ewsx files) can be imported with + Add songs › Import files… in the Songs tab. Their songs and Bible readings are added to the end of the service. Videos and presentations in a schedule aren’t brought across: add them again from the Media tab.","summary":""},{"url":"coming-from-easyworship.html#coming-from-openlp-or-propresenter","page":"Coming from EasyWorship","heading":"Coming from OpenLP or ProPresenter","text":"Coming from OpenLP or ProPresenter","summary":""},{"url":"coming-from-easyworship.html#openlp","page":"Coming from EasyWorship","heading":"OpenLP","text":"OpenLP On the computer that has OpenLP on it, click From OpenLP in the Songs tab. (The button only shows when PraiseAV finds OpenLP’s songs.) Every song comes across with its verses, song order, authors, copyright, CCLI number and chords. If a song has an alternate title or a song book number in OpenLP, it comes after the title in brackets: Abide with Me (Eventide; Our Church Hymns 27) . Search for either one to find it. Songs you already have are skipped, so it’s safe to do again. OpenLP itself isn’t changed. OpenLP on a different computer: in OpenLP, choose Tools › Open Data Folder , and copy the file songs.sqlite from the songs folder onto a USB stick. Then, in PraiseAV, click + Add songs › Import files… and choose that file. Saved OpenLP services ( .osz files) can be imported with Import files… too. Their songs and Bible readings are added to the end of the service. Custom slides, pictures, videos and presentations aren’t brought across: add them again from the Media tab or with + Slides . A service saved by a very old OpenLP (before 2.4) needs opening and saving again in a newer OpenLP first.","summary":""},{"url":"coming-from-easyworship.html#propresenter","page":"Coming from EasyWorship","heading":"ProPresenter","text":"ProPresenter Click + Add songs › Import files… in the Songs tab and choose your ProPresenter songs: .pro or .proBundle files from ProPresenter 7, or .pro6 files from ProPresenter 6. Or drag the whole library folder onto the song list (ProPresenter 7 keeps it in Documents › ProPresenter › Libraries ). Each group of slides ( Verse 1 , Chorus …) becomes a section of the song, each slide stays a slide, and the arrangement becomes the song order. The title, writers, copyright and CCLI number come too. Only the words come across. Fonts, colours and backgrounds stay in ProPresenter: choose a theme in PraiseAV instead.","summary":""},{"url":"coming-from-easyworship.html#where-things-are","page":"Coming from EasyWorship","heading":"Where things are","text":"Where things are In EasyWorship In PraiseAV Schedule The Service column. Double-click songs to add them, or use + Sermon and + Slides . Preview and Live Click a slide to put it on screen. Up next (right) shows exactly what Next will show. Go to the next slide (Page Down, clicker) Next , the → key, Space or Page Down . Clickers work as before. Black, Clear, Logo The same buttons, with the keys B , C and L . Themes The Themes tab. Choose a theme for songs, Bible and sermons, or for one item. See Themes . Media (backgrounds, videos, images) The Media tab. Use as background puts a moving background behind the words. A background or text box on one slide of a song In the song window, click the slide in the preview: give it (or its whole section) its own picture, and extra words at the top or bottom. See A different picture for one verse or the chorus . Audio Music goes in the Media tab too, and plays from Music and audio on the right. See Music and audio . PowerPoint + Add PowerPoint or PDF in the Media tab. Supported Microsoft PowerPoint plays animations and videos first; free LibreOffice Impress can play them when PowerPoint is unavailable. See PowerPoint and PDF . Presentations + Slides opens the slide designer . Foldback (stage display) The Preacher screen: notes, the next slide, the clock, the timer and prompts. See Sermons . Alternate output The Livestream screen: the words in a strip for your ATEM or video mixer. See Livestream . Nursery alerts Congregation alerts , with your own names for the groups. See Congregation alerts . Countdowns Band preacher prompts on the right. See Timers . Announcement loops Set any slides to Loop by itself . See Announcement loops . Saving and transferring schedules Save and Open (in Service ▾ ), on this computer, OneDrive, Google Drive or a USB stick. See Services . Bible (NIV) NIV and more from bible.com in the Bible tab. See The Bible . SongSelect From SongSelect in the Songs tab opens SongSelect in its own window. Sign in with your church’s account, find a song and press Download : it’s added to your songs by itself. See Songs from SongSelect . Reports (song usage) Song usage for CCLI… , under ⋯ in the Songs tab. See CCLI report .","summary":""},{"url":"coming-from-easyworship.html#what-s-different","page":"Coming from EasyWorship","heading":"What’s different","text":"What’s different PraiseAV is free. No subscription, and as many computers as you like. Extra screens are built in. A preacher’s screen, a timer and a livestream feed each go on their own screen, without a subscription or splitter. PowerPoint isn’t required. PDFs open directly; free LibreOffice can turn PowerPoint files into slide pictures and play animations and embedded videos in Impress. Check each guest file on the church computer. Plan at home, run at church: save services in a locally synced OneDrive or Google Drive folder, then open that same synced folder on the church computer. Livestreaming built in: straight to YouTube or Facebook with the stream key, so you can start without OBS or an ATEM, and add them as your church grows. See Going live . A phone remote works in any phone’s web browser, with nothing to install. See Phone remote .","summary":""},{"url":"keyboard-shortcuts.html","page":"Keyboard shortcuts","heading":"","text":"Keyboard shortcuts You can run a whole service from the keyboard, or with a presentation clicker.","summary":"Every key PraiseAV uses: next and back, black, clear, logo, live video, notes pages, saving and help."},{"url":"keyboard-shortcuts.html#running-a-service","page":"Keyboard shortcuts","heading":"Running a service","text":"Running a service Key What it does → ↓ Space Page Down Next slide (at the end of an item, the first slide of the next one) ← ↑ Page Up Previous slide B or . Black screen (press again to come back) C Clear text : hide the words, keep the background L Logo : show your church logo V Live video on or off [ and ] The preacher’s notes: previous and next page Tip: Presentation clickers send Page Down , Page Up and usually B or . for their blank-screen button, so they work with PraiseAV without any setting up.","summary":""},{"url":"keyboard-shortcuts.html#everywhere-else","page":"Keyboard shortcuts","heading":"Everywhere else","text":"Everywhere else Key What it does F1 Help about what you’re doing Ctrl + S Save the service Esc Close a window or dialog Enter (in the Bible tab) Add the reading to the service Shift + Enter (in the Bible tab) Add the reading and show it now","summary":""},{"url":"keyboard-shortcuts.html#in-the-slide-designer","page":"Keyboard shortcuts","heading":"In the slide designer","text":"In the slide designer Key What it does Arrow keys Nudge the selected item (hold Shift to move one pixel) Delete Remove the selected item Ctrl + D Copy the selected item Ctrl + Z Undo Esc Deselect (press again to close) The keys don’t do anything while you’re typing in a box, so you can’t change the screen by accident while searching for a song.","summary":""},{"url":"songs.html","page":"Songs","heading":"","text":"Songs Your songs live in the Songs tab on the left. PraiseAV comes with some well-known hymns, and you can bring in your own from SongSelect, a chord sheet PDF, EasyWorship, OpenLP, ProPresenter, MediaShout, VideoPsalm, OpenSong or a text file.","summary":"Find, add, edit, import and export songs: SongSelect, chord sheet PDFs and song books, EasyWorship, OpenLP, ProPresenter, MediaShout, VideoPsalm, OpenSong, OpenLyrics and plain lyrics files. Labels, slides, song order, backing tracks and videos, and a picture or extra words for one verse or slide."},{"url":"songs.html#find-a-song","page":"Songs","heading":"Find a song","text":"Find a song Type into the search box: a few words of the title , the words , or the author . You don’t need the start of the title: “saved a wretch” finds Amazing Grace .","summary":""},{"url":"songs.html#add-a-song-to-the-service","page":"Songs","heading":"Add a song to the service","text":"Add a song to the service Any of these: Double-click the song. Point at the song and click + . Drag it into the service, to the place you want. New items go just after whatever is selected in the service.","summary":""},{"url":"songs.html#make-a-new-song-or-change-one","page":"Songs","heading":"Make a new song, or change one","text":"Make a new song, or change one Click + Add songs › New song… (top of the Songs tab), or select a song in the service and click Edit song above its slides. You can also point at a song in the list and click ✎ . Box What to put Title The song’s name Author Who wrote it (shown small on the first slide) CCLI song number From SongSelect or the songbook. Needed for your CCLI report . Copyright For example “Public Domain” or “© 2019 Publisher” Song order Optional. The order to sing the sections, for example V1 C V2 C B C . Leave it blank to go in the order written. Backing track or video Optional. Music, or a video that plays behind the words, when the song goes on screen. See Music and audio . Lyrics The words (see below)","summary":""},{"url":"songs.html#writing-the-words","page":"Songs","heading":"Writing the words","text":"Writing the words Put each section’s name in square brackets on its own line: [Verse 1] , [Chorus] , [Bridge] , [Pre-Chorus] , [Tag] . A blank line starts a new slide within a section. Long sections are split into slides of about four lines by themselves, evenly. [Verse 1] Amazing grace! How sweet the sound That saved a wretch like me! [Chorus] ... The preview on the right shows the slides as you type. Click Save song . Tip: With a song order like V1 C V2 C , you only type the chorus once, and it’s shown each time it comes round.","summary":""},{"url":"songs.html#a-different-picture-for-one-verse-or-the-chorus","page":"Songs","heading":"A different picture for one verse or the chorus","text":"A different picture for one verse or the chorus Most of the time a song keeps one look all the way through (its theme ). But you can put one verse, or the chorus, on a picture of its own, and add a few extra words to a slide, like Key change or Men only . In the song window, click the slide in the preview on the right. It opens up underneath. Picture behind the words: choose a picture or video from your Media tab. Choose The song’s usual background to go back. Extra words (optional): type a few words, then choose At the top or At the bottom . They show smaller than the song’s words, in the same lettering. The little picture under the slide shows how it will look. Click Save song . If a section has more than one slide, choose All of Chorus (every slide of it) or Just this slide . With a song order like V1 C V2 C , the chorus shows its picture every time it comes round. The other slides keep the song’s usual look. In the service, slides with their own picture or extra words show them on their slide card, and the congregation screen changes just for those slides. The pictures need to be in the Media tab . A saved service lists them, so you can bring them along on another computer. If the song plays its own video behind the words (a backing video ), or its theme plays a video with sound, that video stays, so the music doesn’t stop. The slide’s own picture shows when the video is switched off for the service. To make a whole slide by hand, with pictures and text boxes anywhere you like, use the slide designer instead.","summary":""},{"url":"songs.html#a-song-in-two-languages","page":"Songs","heading":"A song in two languages","text":"A song in two languages For a church that sings in two languages, a song can show both on the same slide: the second under the first, smaller and in another colour. In the song window, open Words in a second language . Click Copy the headings : the first language s headings ([Verse 1], [Chorus]…) are put in, so you only type the translation under each. Type or paste the translation, with a blank line wherever the first language has one. Click Save song . The preview on the right shows each slide with its translation. PraiseAV pairs them section by section, so a verse that s two slides in one language is two slides in the other. Headings in the second language work too ([Estrofa 1] with [Verse 1]): the second section goes with the second. Which languages show: Show the second language (in the song window): untick it to keep the translation but not show it. Second languages (in the service column, under the times): untick it for a service in one language. Every song then shows only its first language. Languages (in the slides column, for one song in this service): both , first only or second only . This wins over the other two, even while the song is on screen. The congregation screen, the livestream (full screen) and the words on phones show both languages. The band s screen shows the first language, with its chords. How the second language looks (under the first or line by line, its size, colour and italics) is part of the theme . Songs from OpenLP or OpenLyrics files that have a second language keep it.","summary":""},{"url":"songs.html#chords-for-the-band","page":"Songs","heading":"Chords for the band","text":"Chords for the band Click Chords for the band in the song window to add chords. There are two ways. Paste a chord sheet. Most bands already have the chords on their own line above the words, from a website, a document or their own notes: Verse 1 G G7 C G Amazing grace! How sweet the sound D That saved a wretch like me! Copy it and paste it into the chords box. PraiseAV puts each chord into the words, just before the letter it was sitting over, so you see this: {comment: Verse 1} [G]Amazing [G7]grace! How [C]sweet the [G]sound That saved a wretch like [D]me! Headings like Verse 1 , Chorus: or [Chorus] become {comment: …} lines, and a line of chords with no words under it (an intro, say) is kept as it is. A chord sheet you type in yourself is put into the words the same way when you click out of the box. Tip: Text copied out of a PDF usually loses the spaces that line the chords up, so the chords land in the wrong places. For a PDF, import the PDF itself instead: PraiseAV reads where each chord sits on the page. Or start from the words. Click Start from the words to copy the song’s words into the chords box, then type each chord in square brackets just before the word (or syllable) it goes over: [G]Amazing [G7]grace . If the box already has chords in it, PraiseAV asks before replacing them. Chords on the first verse only? Hymn books often print the chords over the first verse and leave them off the rest, because every verse has the same tune. You can do the same: if a verse has no chords of its own (and the same number of lines as the first), the band’s screen shows the first verse’s chords over it, each on the word in the same place in the line. Check it once, as the words of later verses don’t always fall in exactly the same places; give a verse its own chords to change any. Play it in another key. If a song sits too high or too low for your singers, use Band plays it in , just above the chords box. + Higher moves every chord up half a step and − Lower moves it down; or pick a key from the list (the key the chords are written in says as written ). Under the chords box you see the chords the way the band’s screen will show them: Amazing Grace , written in G and played in A, shows [A]Amazing [A7]grace . PraiseAV writes them the way a chord sheet in that key would have them (Bb, not A#), and a chord with a bass note moves both notes, so D/F# becomes E/G#. The chords in the box stay as they were written, so keep typing or changing them in that key. The key you choose is saved with the song: next time it comes up in the key you used last, in saved services and the shared songs folder too. To go back, click Back to G (or whichever key it’s written in). A capo for guitars. Choose Capo for guitars to put a capo on a fret. Each choice says which chord shapes the guitarist plays then: for a song played in A, Capo 2: play G shapes . The band’s screen then shows those shapes, with Capo 2 · G shapes (sounds in A) above the words, so a keyboard player knows the song is really in A. The key and the capo show as {transpose: 2} and {capo: 2} lines at the top of the chords box. The chords then appear above the words on the band’s screen . Songs imported from SongSelect chord charts, chord sheet PDFs, OpenLP and OpenSong bring their chords with them, and Amazing Grace comes with its traditional chords. Print it for the band. Click Print chord sheet… at the bottom of the song window for the song on paper, in the key the band plays it in (with the capo shapes, if it has a capo). To print chord sheets for every song in the service, click Service ▾ › Print and choose Chord sheets (for the band) . See Printing .","summary":""},{"url":"songs.html#delete-a-song","page":"Songs","heading":"Delete a song","text":"Delete a song Open it in the editor and click Delete song . It’s taken out of the library (and today’s service). Saved services keep their own copy, so they still work.","summary":""},{"url":"songs.html#songs-from-songselect","page":"Songs","heading":"Songs from SongSelect","text":"Songs from SongSelect If your church has a SongSelect account (from CCLI), you can find songs there and bring them straight into PraiseAV: In the Songs tab, click From SongSelect . SongSelect opens in its own window. The first time, sign in with your church’s SongSelect email and password. You type them into SongSelect’s own page: PraiseAV never sees them. SongSelect remembers you next time. Find the song as usual, then press SongSelect’s Download and choose the Lyrics or the Chord Sheet . The song is added to your songs by itself. The window’s title, and a message above your song list, say Added “Song title” to your songs , and the song is shown in the list, ready to use. You can download several songs, one after another. Close the SongSelect window when you’ve finished. If you already have the song, it isn’t added twice: the message says it’s already in your songs, and shows you where. A lead sheet (sheet music) can’t be used for words on the screen, so PraiseAV says so. Download the Lyrics or the Chord Sheet instead. Each song is chosen and downloaded by you. PraiseAV doesn’t search SongSelect or download anything by itself, as CCLI’s rules ask. Songs already downloaded from SongSelect can be brought in with Import files… , below.","summary":""},{"url":"songs.html#import-songs","page":"Songs","heading":"Import songs","text":"Import songs Click + Add songs › Import files… in the Songs tab and choose the files (or a zip), or drag files or a whole folder onto the song list. PraiseAV understands: From What to use SongSelect (CCLI) The easiest way is From SongSelect ( above ). Files already downloaded work too: the chord chart (PDF) or the lyrics file ( .txt , or .usr from older downloads). The CCLI number, author, copyright and key come too. Chord sheets (PDF) Chord sheets from a band’s website (Rend Collective’s, for one) and church song books, with the chords on their own line above the words. Each chord goes on the syllable it was printed over, and the title, writers, key and sections ( Verse 1 , Chorus , Repeat Chorus …) come too. A song book becomes one song for each song in it. Sheets with numbers instead of chords (1 4 5) get proper chords if the sheet says its key; if not, just the words come in. Check a song or two afterwards: in song books typed up by hand the chords are sometimes a little out of place. A scanned PDF (a photo of the page) can’t be read. EasyWorship From EasyWorship finds its whole song library on this computer. Or import a Songs.db file, or a schedule ( .ewsx ). See Coming from EasyWorship . OpenLP From OpenLP (shown when OpenLP is on this computer) brings in its whole song library. The alternate title and song book number come after the title in brackets, Abide with Me (Eventide; Our Church Hymns 27) , so you can search for either. Chords, song order, authors, copyright and CCLI number come too. Or import a service ( .osz ): its songs and Bible readings are added to the end of the service. See Coming from OpenLP or ProPresenter . ProPresenter Song files from ProPresenter 7 ( .pro or .proBundle ), 6 ( .pro6 ) or 5, or drag in a whole library folder. Each group ( Verse 1 , Chorus …) becomes a section, and the arrangement becomes the song order. Only the words come in, not the fonts or backgrounds. MediaShout MediaShout’s song library (MediaShout 6 and earlier): import its .mdb database file. Each song’s verses, choruses, bridges and the order they’re sung in come across, with the authors, copyright and CCLI number. MediaShout 7 keeps its songs differently and can’t be read yet. VideoPsalm A VideoPsalm songbook ( .json , in VideoPsalm’s Songbooks folder) brings in every song in it, with authors, copyright, CCLI number and any chords. VideoPsalm doesn’t label its verses, so they come in as Verse 1 , Verse 2 … Rename a chorus in the song editor if you like. OpenSong From OpenSong (shown when OpenSong is on this computer), or import its song files or drag in its Songs folder. OpenLyrics ( .xml ) The free song format that OpenLP, FreeShow, Quelea and PraiseAV’s own export use. A zip, or a folder A zip of song files (a download of chord charts, say), or a whole folder dragged onto the song list. PraiseAV looks through it, including folders inside, and imports every song file it finds. No need to unzip first. A text file A .txt file of lyrics, with section names like Verse 1 or [Chorus] on their own lines. When it’s done, PraiseAV lists what it brought in, what it skipped (songs you already have) and anything that didn’t work, with the reason. A song counts as one you already have when it has the same CCLI number, or the same title and the same words. Small differences don’t matter: commas and full stops, capital letters, the kind of apostrophe, extra spaces and section labels like Verse 1 or Chorus: . So the same hymn copied from another book isn’t added twice. A song with the same title but really different words (a modern version of an old hymn, say) is added as a song of its own. If the song you already have has no chords and the one coming in does, PraiseAV keeps your song (its words, title and author) and adds the chords to it. The results say Already had “Holy, Holy, Holy”; added its chords . If both have chords, yours are kept. Sheet music (notes on staves, with the words under the notes) can’t be imported: PraiseAV needs th","summary":""},{"url":"songs.html#export-songs","page":"Songs","heading":"Export songs","text":"Export songs Your songs are never locked in. In the Songs tab, click ⋯ (next to + Add songs ), then Export songs… , and choose where to put them. PraiseAV makes a folder called PraiseAV songs with the date, and inside it every song twice: Text : a .txt file for each song, easy to read and print, laid out like a SongSelect lyrics file. OpenLyrics : an .xml file for each song, in the free standard format that other worship programs (OpenLP, FreeShow, Quelea…) can import, with verses, song order, authors, copyright and CCLI number. Either kind can be imported back into PraiseAV on another computer. To move everything (services, settings and media too), use a backup instead.","summary":""},{"url":"songs.html#share-songs-with-other-computers","page":"Songs","heading":"Share songs with other computers","text":"Share songs with other computers Use one song library on more than one computer: the church laptop and the office computer, or a volunteer’s computer at home. Type a new song in the office on Wednesday, and it’s on the church laptop on Sunday. You need a folder that both computers can see: OneDrive or Google Drive , signed in to the same account (the church’s) on both computers. Best when one computer is at someone’s home. A shared folder on the church network. A USB stick you move between the computers. Songs go across each time it’s plugged in while PraiseAV is open.","summary":""},{"url":"songs.html#turning-it-on","page":"Songs","heading":"Turning it on","text":"Turning it on In the Songs tab, click ⋯ (next to + Add songs ), then Share songs… . Click Use OneDrive , Use Google Drive or Choose a folder… . PraiseAV makes a folder called PraiseAV Songs there. On the other computer, do the same, and choose the same place. Your songs go into the folder, and the songs already there come into this computer. Songs that both computers have (the same title and words) aren’t doubled.","summary":""},{"url":"songs.html#after-that","page":"Songs","heading":"After that","text":"After that A song added, changed or deleted on one computer is added, changed or deleted on the others by itself. OneDrive and Google Drive can take a few minutes to copy it across the internet. The computers don’t need to be on at the same time. Changes wait in the folder. If the folder can’t be reached (the USB stick isn’t in, or the network is off), PraiseAV carries on as normal. Songs changed meanwhile go across when it’s back. While sharing is on, the Songs tab s ⋯ menu says Sharing songs… . Click it to see when PraiseAV last checked the folder, or to Check now . A song’s backing track and slide timings stay on the computer they were set up on. Pictures on a song’s slides go across, but each computer needs the picture in its own Media tab: without it, that slide shows the theme’s background.","summary":""},{"url":"songs.html#if-two-people-change-the-same-song","page":"Songs","heading":"If two people change the same song","text":"If two people change the same song Sometimes a song is changed on two computers before either has seen the other’s change. PraiseAV then keeps both , so no words are lost. One keeps its name, and the other gets a name like Amazing Grace (changed on OFFICE-PC) . A message above the song list says so. Look at both, keep the one you want, and delete the other.","summary":""},{"url":"songs.html#deleting-a-shared-song","page":"Songs","heading":"Deleting a shared song","text":"Deleting a shared song Delete a song in PraiseAV as usual, and it’s deleted on the other computers too. If someone had changed it on another computer meanwhile, it’s kept, and PraiseAV says so. Don’t delete files from the PraiseAV Songs folder yourself: PraiseAV puts back any song file it finds missing.","summary":""},{"url":"songs.html#turning-it-off","page":"Songs","heading":"Turning it off","text":"Turning it off Click Sharing songs… , then Stop sharing . Every computer keeps all the songs it has, and the folder is left as it is. You can turn it on again later: songs that are the same aren’t doubled. Important: Don’t move PraiseAV’s own data folder into OneDrive, Google Drive or a network folder, and don’t copy PraiseAV’s files there yourself. Those can damage a song library that’s in use. Share songs this way instead: it’s made for this. If PraiseAV finds its own data folder in OneDrive or Google Drive, the Share songs window warns you.","summary":""},{"url":"songs.html#themes-for-a-song","page":"Songs","heading":"Themes for a song","text":"Themes for a song How the words look comes from the theme for songs. To give one song a different look (a Christmas carol, say), pick a theme in the box above its slides. See Themes .","summary":""},{"url":"bible.html","page":"The Bible","heading":"","text":"The Bible The Bible tab finds readings as you type, and puts them on screen with the reference and translation on every slide.","summary":"Find readings by reference or words, choose translations, show the NIV by copying from bible.com, and choose how much goes on each slide."},{"url":"bible.html#find-a-reading","page":"The Bible","heading":"Find a reading","text":"Find a reading Type the reference into the box. Short forms are fine: You type You get jn 3 16 John 3:16 john 3:16-18 John 3:16–18 ps 23 Psalm 23 (the whole psalm) 1 cor 13 4-7 1 Corinthians 13:4–7 rom 8:28-9:1 Romans 8:28 to 9:1 Or type a few words (“good shepherd”) to find verses that contain them. Click a result to open it. Then: Enter or Add to service adds the reading to the service. Shift + Enter or Show now adds it and puts it on screen straight away.","summary":""},{"url":"bible.html#translations","page":"The Bible","heading":"Translations","text":"Translations PraiseAV includes three translations, which work without the internet: BSB : the Berean Standard Bible, modern English (the first choice in English) WEB : the World English Bible RV1909 : the Reina-Valera 1909, in Spanish (the first choice when PraiseAV is in Spanish) The Bibles in the language PraiseAV is set to come first in the lists, and the first of them is used until you choose another. The others are still there, after them. A Spanish Bible s references are in Spanish: Juan 3:16 . For the King James Version (KJV) , use bible.com, the same way as the NIV (below). In the UK the KJV belongs to the Crown, so PraiseAV can’t include the whole of it without permission. Choose the translation in the box next to the search. A reading already in the service can be switched to another translation with the box above its slides.","summary":""},{"url":"bible.html#two-translations-at-once","page":"The Bible","heading":"Two translations at once","text":"Two translations at once For a church in two languages, a reading can show in two translations together: the BSB with the Reina-Valera 1909, say. Each slide has the same verses in both, side by side, and the footer names both ( John 3:16 (BSB · RV1909) ). For one reading: select it in the service, then choose Also: RV1909 (or another) next to its translation. Also: none goes back to one. For every new reading: in Bible settings , choose With every new reading, also show . It s part of the church setup . Side by side or One above the other : in Bible settings , under Two translations on the screen . Second languages in the service column (under the times) turns the second translation off for the whole service, as it does for songs in two languages . With two translations, each slide holds fewer verses, so both stay easy to read. The words on phones show both too. The second translation is one of the Bibles in PraiseAV (not one copied from bible.com).","summary":""},{"url":"bible.html#the-niv-and-other-translations","page":"The Bible","heading":"The NIV and other translations","text":"The NIV and other translations The NIV, ESV, NLT and others can’t be included in PraiseAV, but you can show the verses your service uses: Type the reference, then click NIV and more from bible.com . bible.com opens in its own window, at that chapter. Tap the verses you want (they’re underlined), then press bible.com’s Copy . The verses appear in PraiseAV’s Bible tab. Click Add to service or Show now . You can also copy verses from Bible Gateway or anywhere else, and click Paste verses (or paste into the search box). Include the reference if it’s not from bible.com. Copied verses are kept with the service, so they work at church without the internet. Every slide shows the translation after the reference, like “(NIV)”. That’s what the NIV’s publisher asks for on church slides. Note: Churches can quote up to 500 verses of the NIV without asking permission, with the translation named. A Sunday’s readings are well within that.","summary":""},{"url":"bible.html#bible-settings","page":"The Bible","heading":"Bible settings","text":"Bible settings Open Bible settings at the bottom of the Bible tab: How much of a reading goes on each slide: As much as fits (the default), Shorter slides , or One verse per slide . With every new reading, also show: a second translation that comes with each new reading (see Two translations at once ). Two translations on the screen: Side by side (the default) or One above the other . Translations to show in the lists: untick the ones your church doesn’t use. (The translation in use can’t be hidden.)","summary":""},{"url":"bible.html#how-readings-look","page":"The Bible","heading":"How readings look","text":"How readings look Readings use the theme for Bible verses. Many churches choose High contrast (easy to read) for readings. See Themes .","summary":""},{"url":"services.html","page":"Services","heading":"","text":"Services A service is the order of everything for one meeting: songs, readings, the sermon, videos, notices. It’s the second column of the window.","summary":"Build a service, save it, open it on another computer through OneDrive, Google Drive or a USB stick, and start a new one."},{"url":"services.html#building-a-service","page":"Services","heading":"Building a service","text":"Building a service Songs: double-click in the Songs tab, or drag them in. See Songs . Bible readings: from the Bible tab. See The Bible . The sermon: click + Sermon . See Sermons . Your own slides: click + Slides (notices, a welcome, a verse with a picture). See Slide designer . Videos, pictures, music, PowerPoint: from the Media tab. See Media . New items go just after the selected one. Drag items to change the order. To take one out, point at it and click ✕ . Each item shows what it is: Bible , Sermon , Video , Music or Slides (songs have no label).","summary":""},{"url":"services.html#start-and-end-times","page":"Services","heading":"Start and end times","text":"Start and end times Starts and Ends , in the Service ▾ menu at the top of the column, set when the service begins and finishes. The congregation screen counts down to the start, and the preacher’s screen shows how long is left. See Band and preacher prompts .","summary":""},{"url":"services.html#saving","page":"Services","heading":"Saving","text":"Saving Type a name at the top of the Service column. PraiseAV suggests the coming Sunday’s date. Click Save (or press Ctrl + S ). The words under the name say whether it’s saved. While there are changes not yet saved, a blue Save button shows next to the name. ( Save is always in Service ▾ too.) Tip: PraiseAV also keeps your current service as you work, all the time. If the power goes off, it’s still there when PraiseAV opens again.","summary":""},{"url":"services.html#opening-a-saved-service","page":"Services","heading":"Opening a saved service","text":"Opening a saved service Click Service ▾ , then Open . Your saved services are listed, newest first. Click one to open it. A saved service carries its own copy of every song, so it works on another computer even if that computer doesn’t have the songs: they’re added to its library. Pictures and videos aren’t inside the file (they can be big). If one is missing, PraiseAV tells you which.","summary":""},{"url":"services.html#plan-at-home-run-at-church","page":"Services","heading":"Plan at home, run at church","text":"Plan at home, run at church Using PraiseAV Suite: Plan the service in the Suite and download its .praiseav file. In PraiseAV, choose Service ▾ → Open → More choices → Open from somewhere else and select that file, or save it into the church s shared Services folder. The Suite includes song words that your church has entered, plus the order of service and any key or arrangement selected for that service. A visiting speaker s presentation is represented by a placeholder: add the actual PowerPoint, PDF, or video file in PraiseAV before Sunday. The Suite does not control the live projector. During PraiseAV s first setup, Will you use PraiseAV Suite or prepare services on another computer? offers the church folder guide. Choosing Not now keeps everything on this computer. Open More → Church sharing… whenever you want to set up or change sharing later.","summary":""},{"url":"services.html#the-church-folder","page":"Services","heading":"The church folder","text":"The church folder Keep everything for the church in one folder that every computer can see: in OneDrive, Google Drive or Dropbox, or a shared folder on the church network. Services, songs, media (backgrounds, logos, videos, pictures, music and PowerPoint slides) and the church setup all go there, so what you prepare at home is on the church computer by itself. Click Service ▾ , Open , then Church folder… . Click Use Dropbox , Use OneDrive or Use Google Drive (whichever this computer has), or Choose a folder… . PraiseAV says what it will do. For a new church folder, type the church s name. Click Use this folder . Do the same on the other computer. It finds the church folder and offers Join this church folder . Each computer s own services and media are copied in. Nothing is moved or deleted, and songs both computers have aren t doubled. Both computers need the same OneDrive, Google Drive or Dropbox account signed in, or the folder shared with both. If your church uses PraiseAV Suite, you can sign Google Drive for desktop in with the same church AV Google account that owns the church Firebase project. The Drive folder is optional and carries files; Suite people, rotas and check-ins stay in that church’s Firebase project. PraiseAV does not ask for or store the Google password. Inside, the folder has Services , Songs , Media and Backups (a copy of the weekly automatic backup, so it s safe even if the church computer is lost). Don t rename these folders. Kept on this computer. OneDrive and Dropbox can leave a file online-only , downloading it only when it s opened, and a video that has to download in the middle of a service stalls. So PraiseAV marks the church folder Always keep on this device . Google Drive has no such switch for apps: right-click the PraiseAV folder, then Offline access › Available offline (or choose Mirror files in Google Drive s settings). Ready for Sunday? checks the service s videos and pictures are really on the computer, and Download them now fetches any that aren t. Room for the media. Before using a folder, PraiseAV checks this computer has room to copy its media in, and warns if the media is more than a free account holds (Dropbox 2 GB, OneDrive 5 GB, Google Drive 15 GB). Note: The song library itself stays on each computer: a database in a synced folder can be damaged. Each song is shared as a small file in Songs instead, as with sharing songs . To stop on one computer, click Stop using it on this computer… in the same window. Its services and media are copied back to it, its songs stay in its library, and the folder stays as it is for the other computers.","summary":""},{"url":"services.html#only-services","page":"Services","heading":"Only services","text":"Only services Under More choices in Open , you can keep just the services in OneDrive, Google Drive or another folder, without the songs and media: Use OneDrive or Use Google Drive : services saved at home appear on the church computer by themselves, if both are signed in to the same account. Choose a folder… : anywhere, for example a shared folder. Just this computer : back to normal.","summary":""},{"url":"services.html#with-a-usb-stick","page":"Services","heading":"With a USB stick","text":"With a USB stick Save a copy to a USB stick… saves this service wherever you choose. Open from somewhere else (USB stick)… opens a service file from anywhere.","summary":""},{"url":"services.html#starting-a-new-service","page":"Services","heading":"Starting a new service","text":"Starting a new service Click Service ▾ , then New . If this one has changes that aren’t saved, PraiseAV asks first. Songs stay in your library.","summary":""},{"url":"printing.html","page":"Printing","heading":"","text":"Printing Click Service ▾ at the top of the Service column, then Print . There are three kinds of printout.","summary":"Print a service sheet for the congregation (A4 or a folded A5 booklet), the running order for the team, or chord sheets for the band, or save any of them as a PDF."},{"url":"printing.html#a-service-sheet-for-the-congregation","page":"Printing","heading":"A service sheet, for the congregation","text":"A service sheet, for the congregation A handout with: your church’s logo and name , and the service’s name the order of service the words of each song , laid out by verse and chorus, with the author and CCLI number the Bible readings in full the sermon title, the preacher, and the main points with lines for notes (the points come from the sermon’s slides) notices , if you type some in (they’re kept with the service) the line “Song words reproduced under CCLI Licence No. …”, if you enter your church’s licence number Choose the size: A4 pages : printed as normal. A5 booklet (A4 folded in half) : PraiseAV arranges the pages so that, printed on both sides, the pile folds into a booklet with the pages in order. In the print window, choose print on both sides and flip on short edge . Tick Add a “My notes” page for a ruled page at the end.","summary":""},{"url":"printing.html#a-logo-for-paper","page":"Printing","heading":"A logo for paper","text":"A logo for paper A logo made for the screen often has white or light words, which disappear on white paper. If your church has a version with dark words, use that for printing: in the Media tab, click it and choose Use for printing . See Media . Note: Printing song words for the congregation is covered by a CCLI Church Copyright Licence . Enter your licence number so it’s printed on the sheet. PraiseAV counts printed songs for your CCLI report .","summary":""},{"url":"printing.html#the-running-order-for-the-team","page":"Printing","heading":"The running order, for the team","text":"The running order, for the team A list of everything in the service, with song details. Tick: Include song words : handy for the band Include the preacher’s notes","summary":""},{"url":"printing.html#chord-sheets-for-the-band","page":"Printing","heading":"Chord sheets, for the band","text":"Chord sheets, for the band Big, bold chords over the words, for a music stand. Choose Chord sheets (for the band) in the Print window to print every song in the service that has chords: A short list of the songs comes first, in service order, with the key of each. Songs that don’t have chords yet say no chords yet , so nobody wonders where they went. Each song starts on a new page , with its title, writers, copyright and CCLI song number, and the key in large letters at the top. The chords are in the key the band plays it in , not just the key they’re written in. If the song has a capo , the sheet shows the chord shapes to play and says so at the top: Capo 2 · play G shapes (sounds in A) . Every verse is there with its chords. A verse without chords of its own gets the first verse’s chords, as on the band’s screen, and an intro of chords only is kept. PraiseAV tries not to split a verse or chorus across two pages. To print just one song , open it ( Edit song ) and click Print chord sheet… at the bottom of the song window. It prints the song as it is in the window, in the key and capo you’ve chosen there, even before you save. The key and the capo are set in the song window: see Chords for the band . Note: Printing the words and chords of a song for your own church’s band is covered by your church’s CCLI Church Copyright Licence , for the songs CCLI looks after; chord charts that came from SongSelect are covered by SongSelect’s terms too. If you enter your licence number, it’s printed at the bottom of each song. Old hymns like Amazing Grace are public domain. If you’re not sure about a song, ask CCLI. Each chord sheet you print is counted as printed in your CCLI report , the same as printed words.","summary":""},{"url":"printing.html#saving-as-a-pdf","page":"Printing","heading":"Saving as a PDF","text":"Saving as a PDF Windows’ print window opens after you click Print… . To make a PDF (to email to the band, or put on your website), choose the printer Microsoft Print to PDF .","summary":""},{"url":"ccli-report.html","page":"CCLI report","heading":"","text":"CCLI report If your church has a CCLI Church Copyright Licence , CCLI asks you to report the songs you use. PraiseAV keeps count for you. In the Songs tab, click ⋯ (next to + Add songs ), then Song usage for CCLI… .","summary":"The songs you've used, shown on screen, printed and streamed, ready for your CCLI report, as a spreadsheet or printout."},{"url":"ccli-report.html#what-s-counted","page":"CCLI report","heading":"What’s counted","text":"What’s counted Shown: each song once per service, the first time it goes on screen. Printed: when you print song words (a service sheet, or a running order with words) or chord sheets for the band. Streamed: when a song is shown while the Livestream screen is open or an ATEM is connected. This is for a CCLI Streaming Licence .","summary":""},{"url":"ccli-report.html#choosing-the-dates","page":"CCLI report","heading":"Choosing the dates","text":"Choosing the dates Click Last 6 months , Last 12 months , This year , Last year , or Choose dates .","summary":""},{"url":"ccli-report.html#using-it","page":"CCLI report","heading":"Using it","text":"Using it Save as spreadsheet… saves the list as a file that opens in Excel, LibreOffice or Google Sheets. Print… prints it. Copy the songs into CCLI’s online reporting, or keep the file for your records. Tip: Songs without a CCLI number are marked “none”. Old hymns are often public domain and don’t need reporting. For a song that is covered, add its number in the song editor and it appears in the report. The counts are kept on your computer only. PraiseAV doesn’t send anything to CCLI.","summary":""},{"url":"running-a-service.html","page":"Running a service","heading":"","text":"Running a service On Sunday you mostly need two things: click a slide to put it on screen, and Next to move on.","summary":"Ready for Sunday? before the service, putting slides on screen, Next and Previous, Up next, Black, Clear text and Logo, timed words, videos, the corner logo, clickers, and carrying on if PraiseAV closes."},{"url":"running-a-service.html#ready-for-sunday","page":"Running a service","heading":"Ready for Sunday?","text":"Ready for Sunday? Before the service, click ✓ Ready for Sunday? at the top of the Service column. PraiseAV checks common setup problems and says, in plain words, what to do. It only looks: it never changes anything on the computer. It checks: Screens : the projector is plugged in, Windows is using it as a separate screen (not a copy of the laptop’s), one screen shows the words to the congregation, and the screens aren’t hidden. Files : every video, picture, backing track and PowerPoint in the service is on this computer. One that’s missing (left on another computer, or on a USB stick that isn’t plugged in) is named, with its place in the service. Sound : there are speakers or a sound desk to play through, and where the sound is going. Internet : when the service needs it (live video from YouTube or the web, or ◉ Go live). Power : the laptop is plugged in, not on its battery. Sleep, notifications and updates (warnings): closing the laptop’s lid would put it to sleep; Windows could pop up notifications with a sound; Windows is waiting to restart for an update, or could restart during the service. A ✕ needs putting right before the service. A ! is worth knowing, just in case. A ✓ is fine. Some problems have a button beside them (for example Go to the Media tab for a missing picture). Put things right, then click Check again . When you open a service that’s for today, PraiseAV offers the check once: click Check now . Tip: Run the check when the projector and the sound desk are switched on, about half an hour before the service. That leaves time to find a missing file or a cable.","summary":""},{"url":"running-a-service.html#start-and-end-the-service","page":"Running a service","heading":"Start and end the service","text":"Start and end the service At the start of the service, press ▶ Start the service (under Service ). One press does the usual things: fades out the walk-in music, takes off the logo, black or countdown, and shows the first slide. The button then says ■ End the service . At the end, one press shows the logo, brings the background music back in, and stops the recording (then offers Copy to USB stick ). If you re live, it asks before stopping the livestream. Start and end… , next to the button, chooses what each one does. You can also tick Start recording , Open Go live (you still press Go live now yourself), and, with an ATEM , its livestream and recording. A step that can t happen (no music playing, no ATEM) is skipped, and the line under the button says what was done. These choices are part of the church setup . A MIDI pedal, a Stream Deck button and the phone remote can press Start the service and End the service too. Start the service also folds the songs column away to a thin strip at the left, so the slides and the screens have more room. To add a song during the service, click the strip (or its magnifying glass to search): the column opens in its place, and you can drag the song into the service. ◀ at its top left folds it away again. End the service opens it again. In a narrow window, such as a small laptop s, it starts folded.","summary":""},{"url":"running-a-service.html#putting-something-on-screen","page":"Running a service","heading":"Putting something on screen","text":"Putting something on screen Click an item in the Service column. Its slides appear in the middle. Click a slide. It’s on the screen now: it gets a red border, and the item gets a LIVE label.","summary":""},{"url":"running-a-service.html#next-and-previous","page":"Running a service","heading":"Next and Previous","text":"Next and Previous Next (or → , Space , Page Down , or a clicker) shows the next slide. At the end of an item, Next goes on to the first slide of the next item in the service. Previous (or ← ) goes back.","summary":""},{"url":"running-a-service.html#on-screen-and-up-next","page":"Running a service","heading":"On screen and Up next","text":"On screen and Up next The right-hand column shows: On screen : exactly what the congregation sees now, with a red border. Now and Next : in words. Up next : a small picture of exactly what Next will put on the screen. Tip: Glance at Up next before pressing Next, especially at the end of a song. You’ll never be caught out. Below them are the tools your church uses: at first Congregation alerts and Band preacher prompts . + More tools at the bottom adds Live video , Live captions or Music and audio ; any of them that s turned on shows anyway while it s on. Each folds away to one line until it s wanted. Put them in the order that suits your church with the small ▲ ▼ beside each title: PraiseAV remembers it on this computer.","summary":""},{"url":"running-a-service.html#black-clear-text-and-logo","page":"Running a service","heading":"Black, Clear text and Logo","text":"Black, Clear text and Logo Button Key What happens Black B The screen goes black. Use it between items, or if anything goes wrong. Clear text C The words go, the background stays. Good for a moment of reflection. Logo L Your church logo. Good before and after the service. Press the button again (or the key) to come back. Clicking any slide comes back too. Choose your logo in the Media tab: click a picture and Use as logo . See Media .","summary":""},{"url":"running-a-service.html#timed-words-a-song-that-plays-itself","page":"Running a service","heading":"Timed words (a song that plays itself)","text":"Timed words (a song that plays itself) When a song plays its own recorded music, a backing track or video , the words can move on by themselves in time with it, like a karaoke video. Record the timing once, by pressing Next along with the music: Select the song in the service. Above its slides, click ⏱ Record timing . The song goes on screen from the top, and its video or backing track starts from the beginning. Press Next (or the clicker, a pedal, or the phone remote) in time with the music, just as you would live. At the end, click Done . PraiseAV says how many slide changes it recorded. From then on, Words move by themselves is ticked. When the song comes on screen (from its first slide), the words move on as recorded, and a TIMED label shows. ▶ From the top starts the song and its music again with the timing. If you press Next , Previous or click a slide yourself while it’s timed, the timing stops and you’re in charge again. Black , Clear text and Logo don’t stop it. Not right? Click Record again . Untick Words move by themselves to move the words by hand in this service. The timing is kept with the song , so it’s ready the next time you add the song to a service. It only runs when the song’s backing track or video is playing: if you untick Play the backing track/video (the band is playing it live), the words wait for Next as usual. Note: Timed words are for recorded music, which is the same every time. A live band, which may repeat a chorus or carry on as the Spirit leads, should always have the words moved by a person (or by the worship leader with a pedal or phone ).","summary":""},{"url":"running-a-service.html#videos","page":"Running a service","heading":"Videos","text":"Videos When a video is on screen, buttons appear under On screen : Pause / Play and From the start . It says whether the video loops or plays once. To change that, tick Loop above the video’s slides. Videos play with sound on the projector’s computer (to your sound desk). The video in PraiseAV’s own window is silent.","summary":""},{"url":"running-a-service.html#the-logo-in-the-corner","page":"Running a service","heading":"The logo in the corner","text":"The logo in the corner Some churches like their logo small in a corner all the time, like a TV channel. Open Live video on the right, and tick Church logo in the corner , then choose which corner. The words move to stay clear of it. If your logo has words in it, they’ll be small up there. You can choose a different picture for the corner, such as just your symbol: see Media .","summary":""},{"url":"running-a-service.html#using-a-clicker","page":"Running a service","heading":"Using a clicker","text":"Using a clicker Presentation clickers (like the ones for PowerPoint) work straight away. Forward is Next , back is Previous , and the “blank screen” button is Black . Most work from the back of the room, so the person running PraiseAV can sit with everyone. The phone remote does the same from a phone.","summary":""},{"url":"running-a-service.html#hiding-all-the-screens","page":"Running a service","heading":"Hiding all the screens","text":"Hiding all the screens ⋯ More › Hide the screens (top right) takes all PraiseAV’s screens away, for example to use the projector for something else. While they re hidden, Show the screens in the top bar brings them back. To take just one screen away (say the second monitor, to use it for something else), click Screens… and set that screen to Not used .","summary":""},{"url":"running-a-service.html#if-praiseav-closes-in-the-middle-of-a-service","page":"Running a service","heading":"If PraiseAV closes in the middle of a service","text":"If PraiseAV closes in the middle of a service If PraiseAV closes when nobody asked it to (the power goes, the laptop restarts for an update, or PraiseAV itself goes wrong), open it again. It says PraiseAV closed unexpectedly , and what was on the screen. Click Carry on where I was : the same slide comes back on the screens, with Black or Logo as they were, the same item selected, and the same screens open. It takes a few seconds. Click Start with nothing on the screens instead to begin again with blank screens. Either way the service itself is kept. If you were recording or live on the internet, PraiseAV says so: press ● Record or ◉ Go live again. When you close PraiseAV yourself, it doesn’t ask next time.","summary":""},{"url":"running-a-service.html#the-top-bar-in-a-smaller-window","page":"Running a service","heading":"The top bar in a smaller window","text":"The top bar in a smaller window The top bar always stays one row. When PraiseAV s window is narrower (half the screen, next to something else), it makes room by itself: the key letters on the buttons are hidden, the song s name gets shorter, and ATEM… , Remote and Screens… move into ⋯ More . Widen the window and they come back.","summary":""},{"url":"without-a-volunteer.html","page":"Running a service without an AV volunteer","heading":"","text":"Running a service without an AV volunteer Small churches don’t always have someone for the laptop, and sometimes a volunteer can’t come. PraiseAV can be run from the front with phones: the worship leader runs the songs and readings from their phone (the AV remote ) the preacher puts their sermon on screen and moves their own slides and notes (the preacher remote ) The laptop just sits to one side, plugged in.","summary":"No one free to sit at the laptop? The worship leader runs the words and the preacher runs their sermon, each from their own phone."},{"url":"without-a-volunteer.html#set-it-up-once-before-the-day","page":"Running a service without an AV volunteer","heading":"Set it up once, before the day","text":"Set it up once, before the day Do this when someone is at the laptop, for example at a rehearsal. It only needs doing once for each phone. On the laptop, click Remote (top right), then Switch remote control on . The worship leader scans the code with their phone, types their name and taps Connect . On the laptop, click Allow as AV remote . The preacher does the same. On the laptop, click Allow as preacher remote . PraiseAV remembers both phones. From then on they connect straight away, with nobody needed at the laptop. Remote control also switches itself back on each time PraiseAV opens, if it was on last time. See Phone and tablet remote for more, and what to do if a phone can’t connect.","summary":""},{"url":"without-a-volunteer.html#on-the-day","page":"Running a service without an AV volunteer","heading":"On the day","text":"On the day Plug the laptop in (see the battery below), open PraiseAV, and open the service. Put the welcome slide or logo on screen, then leave the laptop. The worship leader opens the remote on their phone. They tap a song in the service, then Next ▶ for each verse. Black and Logo are there for in between. When it’s time for the sermon , the preacher taps ▶ Start my sermon on their phone. The sermon goes on screen, with their notes on their phone and the preacher’s screen. The preacher uses Slide ▶ and Notes ▶ as they go. (The preacher remote can’t move away from the sermon, so they can’t knock the songs out by accident.) After the sermon, the worship leader taps the next song. Tip: Agree beforehand who taps what, especially who starts the sermon and who takes over afterwards.","summary":""},{"url":"without-a-volunteer.html#the-laptop-stays-awake","page":"Running a service without an AV volunteer","heading":"The laptop stays awake","text":"The laptop stays awake While PraiseAV is showing the screens, or remote control is on, the laptop won’t go to sleep or turn the projector off, however long nobody touches it.","summary":""},{"url":"without-a-volunteer.html#the-battery","page":"Running a service without an AV volunteer","heading":"The battery","text":"The battery When the laptop is running on its battery, a small note at the top of PraiseAV says so ( On battery · 80% ). As it runs down it turns amber, and then red with plug in the laptop . Phones connected to the remote see Laptop battery 25%: plug it in at the top when it gets low, so the people at the front know too. Important: Plug the laptop in before the service. A projector and phones connected for an hour can use a lot of battery.","summary":""},{"url":"without-a-volunteer.html#one-person-leading-worship-and-preaching","page":"Running a service without an AV volunteer","heading":"One person leading worship and preaching","text":"One person leading worship and preaching If the worship leader also preaches, allow their phone as an AV remote . At the bottom of the remote, ⇄ Switch to the preacher’s view gives them their notes, timer and Start my sermon , and ⇄ Switch to the AV view takes them back to the songs. There’s no pairing again. See Phone remote . The same button is handy for demos: one phone shows both remotes.","summary":""},{"url":"sermons.html","page":"Sermons and the preacher’s screen","heading":"","text":"Sermons and the preacher’s screen A sermon has two parts, moved on separately: Slides for the congregation on the front screen: the title, points, pictures. The preacher’s notes , only on the preacher’s screen (usually a TV at the back, where the preacher can see it).","summary":"Add a sermon with slides for the congregation and private notes for the preacher, and show them on the preacher's screen at the back."},{"url":"sermons.html#adding-a-sermon","page":"Sermons and the preacher’s screen","heading":"Adding a sermon","text":"Adding a sermon Click + Sermon at the top of the Service column. Title and Speaker . What the congregation sees first : the church logo, the sermon title, or a sermon picture. Slides : add words (points, verses) and pictures (from the Media tab). They’re shown in order with Next . Notes : the preacher’s notes, only on the back screen. A line with just --- starts a new page. Click Save . To change it later, select the sermon and click Edit sermon above its slides. Tip: If the preacher sends a PowerPoint, add it from the Media tab instead (see PowerPoint and PDF ), and put their notes in a sermon.","summary":""},{"url":"sermons.html#during-the-sermon","page":"Sermons and the preacher’s screen","heading":"During the sermon","text":"During the sermon Next moves the congregation’s slides . Notes ▶ and ◀ Notes (or the ] and [ keys) move the preacher’s notes , separately. Tick Notes follow slides if the preacher wants each slide to bring its own notes page (slide 1 with page 1, and so on). Hide notes puts the back screen back to showing the slides.","summary":""},{"url":"sermons.html#the-preacher-s-screen","page":"Sermons and the preacher’s screen","heading":"The preacher’s screen","text":"The preacher’s screen It shows, from top to bottom: the clock and the timer (see Band and preacher prompts ) any prompt from the desk, like “Wrap up”, big and bright the notes page, with “Page 1 of 4” what’s on screen now for the congregation, and what’s next","summary":""},{"url":"sermons.html#words-for-the-band","page":"Sermons and the preacher’s screen","heading":"Words for the band","text":"Words for the band The same screen is the band’s words during songs. When a song is on screen, the words fill the preacher’s screen, big and bold, with the next line along the bottom, so the worship leader and band can sing without turning round. The sermon notes come back when you go back to the sermon. If a song has chords, they show above the words (in yellow), each over the right syllable:","summary":""},{"url":"sermons.html#choosing-what-it-shows","page":"Sermons and the preacher’s screen","heading":"Choosing what it shows","text":"Choosing what it shows In Screens… , under Preacher screen , tick what you want: During songs, big words for the band, with the next line underneath Chords above the words (for songs that have them; see chords ) Clock Timer (when one is running) Prompts from the desk What’s on the screen and what’s next, along the bottom And the Text size : Normal , Large or Extra large (good for a screen far away, or for older eyes). The preacher’s screen changes as you tick, so you can see the difference. These choices go in the church setup with everything else. To set up the preacher’s screen, see Screens . There’s also a preacher’s phone remote , so the preacher can move their own slides and notes.","summary":""},{"url":"timers-and-prompts.html","page":"Band and preacher prompts: timers and prompts","heading":"","text":"Band and preacher prompts: timers and prompts Open Band preacher prompts in the right-hand column. It has the timer, and the prompts the band and preacher see on their screens.","summary":"Sermon countdowns, counting down to the start of the service, stopwatches, and quiet prompts like \"5 minutes left\" for the preacher."},{"url":"timers-and-prompts.html#service-start-and-end-times","page":"Band and preacher prompts: timers and prompts","heading":"Service start and end times","text":"Service start and end times Under the service’s name are Starts and Ends . Type the times once (10:30 and 11:45, say); new services get the same times, so most weeks there’s nothing to change. Before the service , the congregation screen counts down: Service starts in 4:59 , small in a corner over the notices or the logo. It goes as soon as the first item is put on screen. During the service , the preacher’s and timer screens show Ends 11:45 · 23 min left , amber near the end and red if it runs over. A prompt, 10 minutes to the end of the service , appears on the platform screens at that point. The printed service sheet shows the start time. In Band preacher prompts , under Service times , choose the countdown ( in a corner , full screen , or none ) and when the end prompt comes (or untick it). Nothing stops by itself at the end: if the service runs on as the Spirit leads, the screens just say how far over it is.","summary":""},{"url":"timers-and-prompts.html#timers","page":"Band and preacher prompts: timers and prompts","heading":"Timers","text":"Timers Start sermon timer : type the minutes (say 25 ) and click. It counts down. Count down to time : type a time like 10:30 for “Service starts in…”, before the service. Stopwatch : counts up. While a timer runs: ❚❚ Pause / ▶ Resume , +1 min and −1 min , ↺ Restart and Stop . The timer shows on the preacher’s screen and the timer screen . It turns amber in the last few minutes and red when time is up (it keeps counting, so you can see how far over). Tick Also show the countdown on the congregation screen to show it to everyone, for example “Service starts in 4:59” before the service.","summary":""},{"url":"timers-and-prompts.html#prompts","page":"Band and preacher prompts: timers and prompts","heading":"Prompts","text":"Prompts Prompts are quiet messages for the preacher or the band. They show on the preacher’s screen and timer screen only, never to the congregation. Quick prompts: 5 minutes left , 2 minutes left , Wrap up , Slow down , Mic is off . Or type your own and click Show . Clear takes it away.","summary":""},{"url":"timers-and-prompts.html#the-automatic-prompt","page":"Band and preacher prompts: timers and prompts","heading":"The automatic prompt","text":"The automatic prompt PraiseAV can show “5 minutes left” by itself when the sermon timer gets there. Tick Automatically show and set how many minutes, or untick it to switch it off.","summary":""},{"url":"timers-and-prompts.html#a-timer-screen","page":"Band and preacher prompts: timers and prompts","heading":"A timer screen","text":"A timer screen Some churches put a big timer where the preacher or band can see it (a TV on the floor at the front, say). In Screens… , give that screen the Timer job. See Screens .","summary":""},{"url":"parent-alerts.html","page":"Congregation alerts: parents and notices","heading":"","text":"Congregation alerts: parents and notices Open Congregation alerts in the right-hand column.","summary":"Call a parent from the crèche or children's groups with a number on screen, and scroll short notices like \"car lights left on\"."},{"url":"parent-alerts.html#calling-a-parent","page":"Congregation alerts: parents and notices","heading":"Calling a parent","text":"Calling a parent If you prepare a parent page in PraiseAV Suite, use its public pager reference in the congregation alert. Keep the child s private pickup code at the check-in desk; check that code and the guardian s identity before releasing a child. The Suite prepares text to copy into PraiseAV and does not send the alert itself. Click the group: for example Crèche or Sunday School . Type the child’s number (or name). Click Show on screen (or press Enter ). A banner appears on the congregation screen, over whatever is showing, and the words make room for it: It stays for the number of minutes set (5 to start with), then goes by itself. To take it off sooner, click ✕ next to it in the list. Clear all alerts takes them all away.","summary":""},{"url":"parent-alerts.html#scrolling-notices","page":"Congregation alerts: parents and notices","heading":"Scrolling notices","text":"Scrolling notices Type a short notice (“Would the owner of the blue car please see a steward”) and click Scroll . It scrolls across the bottom of the screen a few times, then stops.","summary":""},{"url":"parent-alerts.html#your-own-group-names","page":"Congregation alerts: parents and notices","heading":"Your own group names","text":"Your own group names Click Settings in Congregation alerts. Rename the groups to what your church calls them: Explorers, Little Lambs, Youth… Add or remove groups. The wording : for example {group}: could the parent of {number} please come . {group} and {number} are filled in. Where on the screen (along the top or bottom) and the Colour . How long a parent alert stays (1 to 10 minutes, or until you clear it), and how many times notices go across. Click Save . Back to the standard wording undoes changes to the wording. Note: Alerts are for the people in the room. They never go out on the livestream or into recordings, so children’s numbers stay private: ◉ Go live and ● Record send the congregation screen without the alerts. (The words still move up to make room while an alert is showing, so viewers just see a little more background for a few minutes.) The Livestream screen (for an ATEM or other mixer) and the screens on the network (for OBS, vMix and other rooms) never have them either, nor do people’s phones with Words on phones . A camera pointed at the screen , or another program recording the whole computer screen (such as OBS’s Display capture ), will see them, just as the room does. Alerts aren’t saved anywhere once they’re cleared.","summary":""},{"url":"controllers.html","page":"MIDI and Stream Deck","heading":"","text":"MIDI and Stream Deck Besides the keyboard, a clicker and the phone remote , PraiseAV can be run from: a MIDI foot pedal, keyboard or pad controller: handy for a worship leader who plays and moves the words themselves a Stream Deck (Elgato’s box of buttons), or Bitfocus Companion Click ⋯ More at the top right of PraiseAV, then MIDI and Stream Deck… .","summary":"Run PraiseAV from a MIDI foot pedal, keyboard or pad controller, or from Stream Deck buttons (or Bitfocus Companion)."},{"url":"controllers.html#midi","page":"MIDI and Stream Deck","heading":"MIDI","text":"MIDI Plug the MIDI device into the computer (USB). A keyboard with a USB socket, a foot pedal with a USB MIDI box, or a small pad controller all work. Tick Use MIDI . PraiseAV lists the devices it found. For each action you want, click Learn , then press the key, pedal or pad for it. It shows what it learnt, for example Note C4 (60) or Controller 64 . The actions: Next , Previous , Black , Clear text , Logo , Notes ▶ , ◀ Notes and Start the sermon . Click ✕ to forget one. PraiseAV remembers the rest on this computer. Tip: A sustain pedal plugged into a keyboard sends Controller 64 : learn it for Next and the keyboard player can move the words with their foot.","summary":""},{"url":"controllers.html#stream-deck","page":"MIDI and Stream Deck","heading":"Stream Deck","text":"Stream Deck","summary":""},{"url":"controllers.html#the-simple-way-hotkeys","page":"MIDI and Stream Deck","heading":"The simple way: hotkeys","text":"The simple way: hotkeys In the Stream Deck app, make Hotkey buttons with PraiseAV’s keys: Button Hotkey Next → (right arrow) Previous ← (left arrow) Black B Clear text C Logo L Notes ▶ / ◀ Notes ] and [ Live video on or off V PraiseAV must be the window in front for hotkeys to work.","summary":""},{"url":"controllers.html#buttons-that-always-work-web-addresses","page":"MIDI and Stream Deck","heading":"Buttons that always work: web addresses","text":"Buttons that always work: web addresses Each action also has a web address, which works even when PraiseAV isn’t in front, or from another computer running Bitfocus Companion. In MIDI and Stream Deck… , click Switch remote control on if it isn’t on already. Click Copy next to an action to copy its address. In the Stream Deck app, use a free web request plugin from the Stream Deck store (search “web request”), and paste the address into a button. In Bitfocus Companion , use the Generic HTTP module with a GET request. The addresses contain a secret key, so only your buttons can use them. Make a new key if you think someone else has the addresses: the old ones stop working. The copied addresses start http://127.0.0.1 , for a Stream Deck plugged into the PraiseAV computer. That listener accepts controller commands only from this computer. For Companion on another computer, use the https:// address shown in the window instead. Companion may need to trust PraiseAV’s local certificate before its web requests work.","summary":""},{"url":"media.html","page":"Media: videos, pictures and backgrounds","heading":"","text":"Media: videos, pictures and backgrounds The Media tab holds your videos, pictures and music: moving backgrounds, your logo, notices, clips.","summary":"Add videos, pictures and music, choose the background behind song words, choose your church logo, folders, slideshows and see-through videos."},{"url":"media.html#free-starter-backgrounds","page":"Media: videos, pictures and backgrounds","heading":"Free starter backgrounds","text":"Free starter backgrounds No backgrounds yet? Click + Free starter backgrounds (in All or Backgrounds ). PraiseAV makes twelve on this computer, in about half a minute, into Backgrounds › Starter : six calm moving loops: Soft Light , Gentle Waves , Aurora , Night Sky , Warm Glow and Morning Mist six still ones: Deep Blue , Royal Purple , Sunrise , Forest , Slate and Warm Sand PraiseAV draws them itself, so they’re free to use for anything, with nothing to license. The moving ones loop smoothly, with no jump.","summary":""},{"url":"media.html#seasonal-backgrounds","page":"Media: videos, pictures and backgrounds","heading":"Seasonal backgrounds","text":"Seasonal backgrounds Click + Seasonal backgrounds in All or Backgrounds for Easter, Pentecost, Harvest, Christmas/Advent, New Year and conferences. Each occasion has a still picture and a gently moving version, saved in Backgrounds › Seasonal . PraiseAV draws them on this computer, so no stock-media subscription or artwork licence is needed. Making the moving versions may take a few minutes; anything already made is kept if you stop and try again. The matching themes use the still pictures for dependable readability. To put movement behind one song, copy its theme and choose that occasion’s .webm video under Behind the words . The artwork contains no words, so your own event title and lyrics stay clear.","summary":""},{"url":"media.html#where-the-files-live","page":"Media: videos, pictures and backgrounds","heading":"Where the files live","text":"Where the files live PraiseAV keeps media in one folder: Documents › PraiseAV Media to start with. The path is shown under the buttons. Inside are folders for each kind: Backgrounds : what goes behind song words and verses. Logos : your church logo. Videos and Pictures : clips, notices, photos. Music and Audio : walk-in music, backing tracks. The chips along the top ( All , Backgrounds , Logos …) show one folder at a time. Open folder opens it in File Explorer. Change folder… uses a different folder, for example where your old EasyWorship backgrounds are, or a shared folder.","summary":""},{"url":"media.html#adding-files","page":"Media: videos, pictures and backgrounds","heading":"Adding files","text":"Adding files Click + Add videos, pictures or music , or drag files from File Explorer onto the list. They’re copied into the media folder, so they still work after a USB stick is unplugged. Music goes into Music and Audio by itself. Kind Files that work Videos MP4, M4V, MOV, WEBM, OGV Pictures JPG, PNG, GIF, WEBP, BMP, SVG Music MP3, M4A, AAC, WAV, OGG, OPUS, FLAC Careful: Some old video files (WMV, AVI, or ProRes from a Mac) won’t play. PraiseAV says so if one doesn’t. The free HandBrake app converts them to MP4, which always works.","summary":""},{"url":"media.html#using-a-file","page":"Media: videos, pictures and backgrounds","heading":"Using a file","text":"Using a file Click a file to choose it. Buttons appear underneath: Add to service (or double-click the file, or drag it into the service). Use as background : shows it behind song words and Bible verses. It gets a Background label. Use as logo : shown when you press Logo ( L ). It gets a Logo label. Use in the corner : a different picture for the small logo in the corner of the screen. It gets a Corner logo label. Use for printing (pictures): a different picture for printed service sheets. It gets a Print logo label. Play over the screen (videos): see below. ▶ Play now (music): see Music and audio .","summary":""},{"url":"media.html#your-church-background","page":"Media: videos, pictures and backgrounds","heading":"Your church background","text":"Your church background A gentle moving background behind the words looks good and is easy to read. Choose one in Backgrounds , then Use as background . Themes can use a different one (see Themes ).","summary":""},{"url":"media.html#your-church-logo","page":"Media: videos, pictures and backgrounds","heading":"Your church logo","text":"Your church logo Put your logo in Logos , click it, then Use as logo . A PNG with a see-through background looks best. It also appears on printed service sheets and can sit small in a corner of the screen (see Running a service ).","summary":""},{"url":"media.html#a-different-logo-for-the-corner","page":"Media: videos, pictures and backgrounds","heading":"A different logo for the corner","text":"A different logo for the corner A logo with words looks good full screen, but in the small corner the words become hard to read. Many churches use just their symbol there (the cross, the heart, the dove…). Make a picture of just the symbol, and add it to Logos . Click it, then Use in the corner . The full logo is still used for Logo ( L ) and the service sheet. Stop using in the corner puts the full logo back in the corner.","summary":""},{"url":"media.html#a-logo-for-printing","page":"Media: videos, pictures and backgrounds","heading":"A logo for printing","text":"A logo for printing Logos for screens usually have light words, made to stand out on a dark background. On white paper those words disappear. Many churches have a second version with dark words for paper. Add the dark version to Logos . Click it, then Use for printing . Printed service sheets then use it, and the screens keep the light one. Stop using for printing puts the church logo back on the sheets.","summary":""},{"url":"media.html#sing-along-with-a-worship-video","page":"Media: videos, pictures and backgrounds","heading":"Sing along with a worship video","text":"Sing along with a worship video A worship video of a band playing, with no words on it? Put the song’s words over it so everyone can sing along: Click the video, then Sing along with a song… . Type the song’s title (or some words), and click it in the list. The song must be in the Songs tab already. The song goes into the service with the video as its backing video . When it’s on screen, the video plays behind the words with its sound. Move the words with Next , or record the timing once so they move by themselves. To show the video on its own, without words, add the video itself to the service instead.","summary":""},{"url":"media.html#folders-inside-folders","page":"Media: videos, pictures and backgrounds","heading":"Folders inside folders","text":"Folders inside folders In Backgrounds (or any folder), click + Folder to make one inside it, for example Christmas or Easter . Then click it and add files. All in Backgrounds shows them all again.","summary":""},{"url":"media.html#a-slideshow-of-pictures","page":"Media: videos, pictures and backgrounds","heading":"A slideshow of pictures","text":"A slideshow of pictures Open a folder with several pictures and click Make a slideshow of these pictures . They go into the service as one item that moves on by itself every 8 seconds. See Announcement loops .","summary":""},{"url":"media.html#see-through-videos-over-the-screen","page":"Media: videos, pictures and backgrounds","heading":"See-through videos over the screen","text":"See-through videos over the screen Some videos are made to go on top : confetti, snow, falling leaves, light leaks. Choose one and click Play over the screen . It plays over whatever is showing, and the words stay. Under On screen , tick Loop or click Stop . These need a video with a see-through background (usually WEBM). Ordinary videos would cover everything.","summary":""},{"url":"media.html#videos-in-the-service","page":"Media: videos, pictures and backgrounds","heading":"Videos in the service","text":"Videos in the service A video in the service shows as one slide. Click it to play. See Running a service . A YouTube or Vimeo video, or a web page, can go in the service too, without downloading it: see Web pages and YouTube videos .","summary":""},{"url":"media.html#good-background-clips","page":"Media: videos, pictures and backgrounds","heading":"Good background clips","text":"Good background clips If you’re choosing or buying moving backgrounds: 1920 × 1080 (Full HD), MP4 (H.264). 4K looks no better on most projectors and makes the computer work harder. Seamless loops of 15 to 60 seconds, so there’s no jump when they go round. Slow and calm , dark or soft colours, nothing busy in the middle where the words go. Keep them under about 50 MB each. Free sources include Pixabay and Pexels (search “abstract loop” or “worship background”). Check each site’s licence.","summary":""},{"url":"web-pages.html","page":"Web pages and YouTube videos","heading":"","text":"Web pages and YouTube videos A YouTube video, a Vimeo video or any web page can be an item in the service, like a song or a video. It fills the congregation screen when you put it on.","summary":"Show a YouTube or Vimeo video, or any web page, full screen as part of the service, with buttons to play, pause and scroll it."},{"url":"web-pages.html#add-one","page":"Web pages and YouTube videos","heading":"Add one","text":"Add one Open the Media tab and click + Web page or YouTube . Paste the link: a YouTube video: youtube.com/watch?v=… , youtu.be/… , youtube.com/live/… or a Short; a Vimeo video; any web page, starting https:// . Give it a name for the service list if you like ( Mission video , Notices page ). Without one it s called YouTube video , Vimeo video or the website s name. Click Add to service . It goes after the item that s selected, with a Web label. A YouTube channel or a page of several videos can t be shown: copy the link of the video itself (click Share under the video on YouTube).","summary":""},{"url":"web-pages.html#show-it","page":"Web pages and YouTube videos","heading":"Show it","text":"Show it Click its slide, as with anything else. The video or page fills the congregation screen. The On screen picture shows its name rather than a second copy, so the church s internet only plays it once and the sound only comes from one place. Above the slide are buttons that work on the screen, since nobody can reach the projector: Button What it does ⏯ Play / Pause Pauses the YouTube or Vimeo video, or carries on. ▲ Up and ▼ Down A web page: moves it up or down a screenful, for a long page of notices or words. ⏯ Video A video on a web page: pauses it, or carries on. ⟳ Reload Loads it again from the start, if it stopped or something went wrong. Open in browser Opens it in your web browser, to check it before the service. Black covers it as it does anything else, and Logo shows your logo instead. Click the slide again to bring it back.","summary":""},{"url":"web-pages.html#things-to-know","page":"Web pages and YouTube videos","heading":"Things to know","text":"Things to know It needs the internet , during the service. Ready for Sunday? checks the connection when the service has a web page or online video in it. For a video you ll show every week, it s safer to download it and add it as an ordinary video under + Add videos, pictures or music (when you have the right to). Nothing is shown over a YouTube or Vimeo video. That s YouTube s rule. If a parent alert goes up, the video gets a little smaller to make room for it underneath. Over a web page, alerts show as usual. Advertisements: YouTube may show adverts before or during a video. Check it in your browser beforehand, or use a video your church uploaded. Pages that need you to sign in show their sign-in page instead, because the screen isn t signed in to anything. Copyright: showing a video in church needs the right licence, such as a CCLI Video Licence. Videos you made yourselves are fine. Web pages on the church s own network (another computer running a countdown, say) can start http:// . Anything on the internet needs https:// .","summary":""},{"url":"music-and-audio.html","page":"Music and audio","heading":"","text":"Music and audio PraiseAV can play music through the computer’s sound output, usually a cable to the sound desk.","summary":"Walk-in music, music cues in the service, backing tracks that start with a song, fade out, volume and loop."},{"url":"music-and-audio.html#walk-in-music","page":"Music and audio","heading":"Walk-in music","text":"Walk-in music Put your music in the Media tab, in Music and Audio (click + Add videos, pictures or music ). Click Music and Audio , then ▶ Play all . The tracks play one after another. Or click one track and ▶ Play now .","summary":""},{"url":"music-and-audio.html#controlling-it","page":"Music and audio","heading":"Controlling it","text":"Controlling it Open Music and audio in the right-hand column (the first time, tick it under + More tools at the bottom of the column; it also shows by itself while something s playing): ▶ Play / ❚❚ Pause . Fade out : turns it down gently over 3 seconds, then stops. Use it when the service starts. ■ Stop : stops at once. Skip ▶▶ : next track. Drag the bar to jump to a point in the track. Volume , and Loop to go round again when it ends. The top line always says what’s playing and how long is left.","summary":""},{"url":"music-and-audio.html#spotify-or-another-music-app","page":"Music and audio","heading":"Spotify (or another music app)","text":"Spotify (or another music app) Many churches play a Spotify playlist before and after the service, and for quiet moments during it. Keep using Spotify: PraiseAV works it for you, so nobody has to switch away during a service. Open Spotify on the PraiseAV computer and start a playlist (for example “Pre-service”). Spotify can sit behind PraiseAV, or be minimised. In PraiseAV, the ♪ music strip in the top bar shows the song that’s playing, with ❚❚ (pause), ▶ (play) and Fade out (or Fade in ) always to hand. Click the song for all the controls: Fade out : turns it down gently, then pauses. Use it as the service begins, or after a reflective moment. ▶ Fade in : starts quietly and comes up gently. ❚❚ Pause and ▶ Play : straight away. Next song ▶▶ : skips to the next one. The Spotify volume slider changes only Spotify, not PraiseAV’s videos or the rest of the computer. Background music also says where the sound is going : this computer’s speakers, a USB sound box (usually the sound desk), or a screen. If it’s going to a screen (a projector or a monitor, whose speakers are often off), it warns you in orange. Choose where it should go in Sound goes to . That moves all this computer’s sound, PraiseAV’s videos too, so it’s the one place to set it. PraiseAV also fades it out by itself : When PraiseAV plays a video or music with sound, so a countdown video or a song’s backing track never plays on top of the playlist. When the service starts , at the Starts time in Service ▾ . Untick either one in Background music if you don’t want it. Tip: if Windows ever turns the music down on its own (it does while a microphone is in use, for example while PraiseAV records the sound desk), Background music shows Stop Windows doing that . One click, and it won’t happen again. It works with other music apps too (for example a playlist in a web browser), but Spotify is the one it looks for first. Tip: in Spotify’s settings, turn Autoplay off (so it doesn’t carry on into random songs when the playlist ends) and Crossfade on. With Spotify Premium, download the playlist, so a Wi-Fi drop can’t stop the music, and there are no adverts. Music licences: Spotify’s own terms are for personal listening. Playing recorded music in church can need a music licence (rules for worship differ from events and cafés, and from country to country). Ask your denomination or your CCLI or music licence provider.","summary":""},{"url":"music-and-audio.html#music-in-the-service","page":"Music and audio","heading":"Music in the service","text":"Music in the service Drag a music file from the Media tab into the service, for example a track for the offering. It shows with a Music label. Click it and press the big ▶ Play button. The screens don’t change, so you can play music under a slide.","summary":""},{"url":"music-and-audio.html#backing-tracks-and-videos","page":"Music and audio","heading":"Backing tracks and videos","text":"Backing tracks and videos If the band is away, a song can have a backing track (an MP3, say) or a video (a worship video of a band playing) that plays when the song goes on screen. Add the music or video in the Media tab. In the Songs tab, choose the song and click Edit song . Under Backing track or video , choose the file. Save. When the song goes on screen, the track or video starts from the beginning. A video plays behind the words, with its sound , so everyone can sing along, like a karaoke video. When you move on to the next item, it stops. For a video there’s a quicker way: in the Media tab, click the video, then Sing along with a song… and choose the song. See Media . A week when the band plays it live? Above the song’s slides, untick Play the backing track (or Play the backing video ). The song is then just the words, moved with Next as usual. It only changes that service. The words can also move on by themselves in time with the track or video: see Timed words . Note: Spotify, Apple Music and YouTube Music can’t be played through PraiseAV: their apps don’t allow it. Buy the tracks as MP3s or use your own recordings, and check your licence covers playing them in church (in the UK, CCLI can arrange the PRS for Music Church Licence).","summary":""},{"url":"music-and-audio.html#the-sound","page":"Music and audio","heading":"The sound","text":"The sound Music plays through Windows’ default sound output. To choose which (say, the headphone socket to the sound desk, or the HDMI to the projector), click the speaker icon by the clock at the bottom right of Windows. Videos’ sound comes from the congregation screen’s window, through the same output.","summary":""},{"url":"powerpoint-and-pdf.html","page":"PowerPoint and PDF","heading":"","text":"PowerPoint and PDF A visiting speaker brings slides on a USB stick? Add them to the service.","summary":"Bring in a guest speaker's PowerPoint or PDF. Play animations and embedded video with supported Microsoft PowerPoint or free LibreOffice Impress."},{"url":"powerpoint-and-pdf.html#adding-slides","page":"PowerPoint and PDF","heading":"Adding slides","text":"Adding slides In the Media tab, click + Add PowerPoint or PDF and choose the file. Or drag the file onto the service. PraiseAV makes a picture of each slide. It says how far it’s got (“Making slides… 4 of 12”), then adds the slides to the service. Wait for that import to finish before removing the speaker’s USB stick. PraiseAV keeps its own copy of the PowerPoint alongside the slide pictures. Pictures and videos embedded inside the PowerPoint file travel with that copy. A video linked to another file or website still needs that file or an internet connection at church. Click a slide to put it on screen, and use Next as normal. The slides are kept in the Media tab under Presentations , so you can add them to another service.","summary":""},{"url":"powerpoint-and-pdf.html#which-files","page":"PowerPoint and PDF","heading":"Which files","text":"Which files PDF : opens directly, provided the file itself is valid. PowerPoint (PPTX, PPT, PPS, PPSX), LibreOffice (ODP) and Keynote (KEY): need a supported desktop Microsoft 365 or Office 2024 PowerPoint installation, or the free LibreOffice , on this computer. PraiseAV uses PowerPoint first (closest to how the slides were made), otherwise LibreOffice. Office 2021 and older are no longer selected for live PowerPoint playback. If neither is installed, PraiseAV offers to open the LibreOffice download page. Install it once, then add the PowerPoint again. Check the result of an automatic LibreOffice import. A PowerPoint file can look different when another program renders it: fonts, text boxes, layered pictures and effects may shift. PraiseAV uses LibreOffice to make slide pictures and, when PowerPoint is unavailable, to play the original file in Impress. Preview every imported slide and test the live show before the service, especially a visiting speaker s file. Using Apache OpenOffice? PraiseAV does not currently detect Apache OpenOffice as an automatic converter. Open the presentation in OpenOffice Impress, choose File › Export as PDF , then add the PDF to PraiseAV. This needs no Microsoft PowerPoint. The PDF shows the text and pictures as still slides; animations and embedded videos do not play. If the speaker needs a video, ask for the video file separately and add it from the Media tab. Using ONLYOFFICE Desktop Editors? PraiseAV does not currently detect it as an automatic converter either. Open the presentation in ONLYOFFICE, choose File › Save as › PDF , then add the PDF to PraiseAV. Check every exported page on the church computer. In our test, ONLYOFFICE included a hidden slide in the PDF, so remove any unwanted pages before the service. Animations and embedded media need another playback route. See ONLYOFFICE s presentation export guide . Tip: Ask speakers to send a PDF too. It imports without another office program and is a useful fallback if a presentation looks different on the church computer.","summary":""},{"url":"powerpoint-and-pdf.html#animations-and-videos-with-powerpoint-or-libreoffice-impress","page":"PowerPoint and PDF","heading":"Animations and videos (with PowerPoint or LibreOffice Impress)","text":"Animations and videos (with PowerPoint or LibreOffice Impress) PraiseAV uses a supported Microsoft 365 or Office 2024 PowerPoint installation first. If that is unavailable or cannot open a particular file, it uses LibreOffice Impress if installed. Either program can play the presentation s interactive parts: Animations work: points appear one at a time. Transitions between slides work (a fade, a wipe…). Videos inside the slides play, with their sound. For a visiting speaker, test one slide with a video and its sound on the church computer before the service, then remove the USB stick and test it again. Video playback depends on the chosen presentation program and the formats installed on that computer. Ask the speaker to embed videos, use MP4 (H.264 video and AAC audio) where possible, and bring a PDF copy as a fallback. A PDF preserves pictures and text but cannot play its embedded PowerPoint animations or videos. Impress may render PowerPoint animations, triggers or layouts differently, so test the whole presentation. Above the slides it says Plays in PowerPoint or Plays in LibreOffice Impress . Put the first slide on screen and press Next as usual (the arrow keys, Space, a clicker, or the phone remote). Each press advances an animation or a slide. Back goes back a step. While PowerPoint plays, the line above the slides also shows the animation step. Impress shows the slide number. After the last slide, Next goes on to the next item in the service, as with songs. Putting anything else on screen (a song, a reading) closes the slide show by itself. Black and Logo work as normal, and so do parent alerts . Good to know: The presentation opens out of sight when you click it in the service, so it starts quickly when you show it. The very first time can take a few seconds; the screen stays black meanwhile, so nobody sees all the points before the speaker gets to them. Don’t close PowerPoint or LibreOffice while the slides are showing. PraiseAV controls the show for you. The sound of videos in the slides comes out of the computer’s normal sound output, the same as PraiseAV’s own videos. Slides that move on by themselves (if the speaker set timings in PowerPoint) do so here too, and the slide cards follow. If PowerPoint cannot play the file, PraiseAV tries Impress. If neither can play it, PraiseAV says so above the slides and shows the pictures instead.","summary":""},{"url":"powerpoint-and-pdf.html#what-the-livestream-recording-and-other-screens-show","page":"PowerPoint and PDF","heading":"What the livestream, recording and other screens show","text":"What the livestream, recording and other screens show ◉ Go live and ● Record show the slides exactly as the room sees them, animations and videos included. (The sound of a video in the slides reaches the livestream the way your other sound does: through the mixer or microphone.) The preacher’s screen says which slide is on and what’s next, as it does for pictures. Screens on the network (such as OBS) show each slide as a still picture: the slide as it looks when all its points are in.","summary":""},{"url":"powerpoint-and-pdf.html#without-powerpoint-or-libreoffice-impress","page":"PowerPoint and PDF","heading":"Without PowerPoint or LibreOffice Impress","text":"Without PowerPoint or LibreOffice Impress If neither presentation program can play the file, or if you add a PDF, each slide is a still picture, so: Animations (points appearing one by one) and transitions don’t happen. Each slide shows as it looks at the end. Videos inside the PowerPoint don’t play. Ask for the video file separately and add it from the Media tab. Above the slides it says Shown as pictures . The PowerPoint file itself is kept with the pictures in the media folder, so a computer with PowerPoint or LibreOffice Impress can try to play it live.","summary":""},{"url":"powerpoint-and-pdf.html#speaker-s-notes","page":"PowerPoint and PDF","heading":"Speaker’s notes","text":"Speaker’s notes The speaker’s notes aren’t brought in. Paste them into a sermon to show on the preacher’s screen.","summary":""},{"url":"powerpoint-and-pdf.html#moving-on-by-itself","page":"PowerPoint and PDF","heading":"Moving on by itself","text":"Moving on by itself Tick Loop by itself above the slides to make them move on every few seconds and go round again: good for a notices presentation before the service. See Announcement loops .","summary":""},{"url":"slide-designer.html","page":"Slide designer","heading":"","text":"Slide designer For a welcome slide, notices, or a verse over a picture, make your own slides.","summary":"Make your own slides: a welcome, notices, a verse with a picture. Words, pictures, boxes and backgrounds, with ready-made layouts."},{"url":"slide-designer.html#making-slides","page":"Slide designer","heading":"Making slides","text":"Making slides Click + Slides at the top of the Service column. The slide designer opens. On the left : the slides. Click one to change it. Point at a slide for ↑ and ↓ (move), ⧉ (copy) and ✕ (delete). In the middle : the slide itself. On the right : settings for whatever you’ve clicked. Type a name at the top (“Welcome”, “Notices”), then Save slides . They go into the service. To change them later, select the item and click Edit slides above its slides.","summary":""},{"url":"slide-designer.html#a-new-slide","page":"Slide designer","heading":"A new slide","text":"A new slide Click + New slide and pick a layout to start from: Title Title and text Picture with caption Words on a band Big verse Blank","summary":""},{"url":"slide-designer.html#adding-things","page":"Slide designer","heading":"Adding things","text":"Adding things At the top: + Words , + Picture , + Box . Drag to move. Drag a corner to resize. Lines appear when things line up with the middle or each other. Arrow keys nudge (hold Shift for one pixel at a time). Delete removes, Ctrl + D copies, Ctrl + Z undoes (or ↶ Undo ).","summary":""},{"url":"slide-designer.html#words","page":"Slide designer","heading":"Words","text":"Words Click the words, then type in the box on the right. Choose the Font , Size , colour, B (bold), I (italic), Shadow , and Left , Centre or Right .","summary":""},{"url":"slide-designer.html#pictures","page":"Slide designer","heading":"Pictures","text":"Pictures Choose which picture (from the Media tab), and Whole picture (shows all of it) or Fill the box (crops to fit). Rounded corners if you like.","summary":""},{"url":"slide-designer.html#boxes","page":"Slide designer","heading":"Boxes","text":"Boxes A coloured box, for example a band behind words. Choose the colour, See-through and Rounded corners .","summary":""},{"url":"slide-designer.html#arrange","page":"Slide designer","heading":"Arrange","text":"Arrange Centre across , Centre down , Bring forward , Send back , Copy , Delete .","summary":""},{"url":"slide-designer.html#the-slide-background","page":"Slide designer","heading":"The slide background","text":"The slide background Click an empty part of the slide. On the right, under Slide background : The usual background : your church background (from the Media tab). A colour . A picture or video from the Media tab.","summary":""},{"url":"slide-designer.html#moving-on-by-itself","page":"Slide designer","heading":"Moving on by itself","text":"Moving on by itself For notices before the service, tick Loop by itself above the slides. See Announcement loops .","summary":""},{"url":"announcement-loops.html","page":"Announcement loops","heading":"","text":"Announcement loops Before the service, it’s good to have the notices going round on the screen by themselves while people come in.","summary":"Notices that move on by themselves and go round again before the service, from your own slides, pictures or a PowerPoint, and can start by themselves at a set time."},{"url":"announcement-loops.html#making-one","page":"Announcement loops","heading":"Making one","text":"Making one Any of these can loop: Your own slides : click + Slides and make them. See Slide designer . Pictures : in the Media tab, open a folder of notice pictures and click Make a slideshow of these pictures . A PowerPoint or PDF of notices. See PowerPoint and PDF .","summary":""},{"url":"announcement-loops.html#turning-on-the-loop","page":"Announcement loops","heading":"Turning on the loop","text":"Turning on the loop Select the item in the service. Above its slides: Tick Loop by itself . Choose how long each slide stays: every 5 s up to every 60 s . Eight to ten seconds suits most notices. Click the first slide to start. A LOOPING label shows while it goes round. It stops when you move on to anything else. Black or Logo pauses it (it says PAUSED ). Press the button again to carry on.","summary":""},{"url":"announcement-loops.html#starting-by-itself","page":"Announcement loops","heading":"Starting by itself","text":"Starting by itself The notices can start on their own before the service, so nobody has to be at the computer at 9:45. Turn on Loop by itself (above). Tick Start by itself at and set the time. It suggests 15 minutes before the service s start time. If the service has a start time (under its name), you can also tick Then the logo at 10:30 : at the start time the notices stop and your logo shows, ready for the first song. The service list shows a clock badge, ⏰ 9:45 , on a loop that starts by itself. Some things to know: Leave PraiseAV open, with the screens on. The computer won t go to sleep while a loop is waiting to start. It only starts on the day of the service : a service named Sunday 25 October 2026 starts on that Sunday, not when you open it to plan on Wednesday. A service without a date in its name starts any day. It won t take over the screens if something else is showing (a rehearsal, say). Instead it says it didn t start, and you can click its first slide yourself. It starts once that day. If you stop it, it stays stopped. If PraiseAV was opened a little late, up to half an hour, it still starts.","summary":""},{"url":"announcement-loops.html#with-walk-in-music","page":"Announcement loops","heading":"With walk-in music","text":"With walk-in music Start the loop. In the Media tab, open Music and Audio and click ▶ Play all . When the service starts, click Fade out under Music and audio, then click your first song.","summary":""},{"url":"announcement-loops.html#a-countdown-too","page":"Announcement loops","heading":"A countdown too","text":"A countdown too To show “Service starts in 4:59” with the notices, start a Count down to time in Band preacher prompts, and tick Also show the countdown on the congregation screen . See Band and preacher prompts .","summary":""},{"url":"themes.html","page":"Themes: how the words look","heading":"","text":"Themes: how the words look A theme is how the words look: the font, size, colour and what’s behind them.","summary":"Fonts, sizes, colours, outlines, where the words sit and what goes behind them, for songs, Bible verses and sermons. How slides change, and words appearing line by line or word by word."},{"url":"themes.html#the-built-in-themes","page":"Themes: how the words look","heading":"The built-in themes","text":"The built-in themes Classic : the starting theme. Bold outline : thick letters with an outline, easy to read over busy backgrounds. Elegant : a serif font. High contrast (easy to read) : for people who find the screen hard to read. Words at the bottom : leaves the picture clear above. Words at the top : for rooms where heads, the band or the platform hide the bottom of the screen. Easter, Pentecost, Harvest, Christmas / Advent, New Year and Conference : occasion themes with matching seasonal backgrounds . Each starts with a suitable dark colour until you make its artwork in the Media tab. Choosing one does not change the church s usual theme unless you click Use for .","summary":""},{"url":"themes.html#choosing-a-theme-for-songs-bible-verses-and-sermons","page":"Themes: how the words look","heading":"Choosing a theme for songs, Bible verses and sermons","text":"Choosing a theme for songs, Bible verses and sermons Under each theme, Use for: has Songs , Bible and Sermons . Click to make that theme the one they get. For example, songs in Classic and Bible verses in Elegant.","summary":""},{"url":"themes.html#one-item-in-a-different-theme","page":"Themes: how the words look","heading":"One item in a different theme","text":"One item in a different theme Select an item in the service. Above its slides, choose a Theme: from the list. Only that item changes.","summary":""},{"url":"themes.html#a-video-behind-one-song-communion-for-example","page":"Themes: how the words look","heading":"A video behind one song (communion, for example)","text":"A video behind one song (communion, for example) Tip: For a worship video with its own music, the easiest way is to make it the song’s backing video : see Sing along with a worship video . A theme is best for a calm, silent video behind several songs. For a reflective moment, put a calm video behind just one song: Add the video in the Media tab (a candle, bread and wine, or a starter background ). Click + New theme , name it (“Communion”), and set Behind the words to A picture or video , choosing the video. In the service, select the song and choose Theme: Communion above its slides. The video loops behind the words for that song only. Sound: a background video is silent unless you tick Play the video’s sound in the theme (it shows when the background is a video). Then the video’s own sound plays through the projector computer’s sound, starting from the beginning each time a song with that theme comes on. That’s useful for a video with its own music, and the words can even move by themselves in time with it . Or keep the video silent and give the song a backing track , or play music from Music and audio.","summary":""},{"url":"themes.html#one-verse-or-the-chorus-on-its-own-picture","page":"Themes: how the words look","heading":"One verse or the chorus on its own picture","text":"One verse or the chorus on its own picture A theme gives the whole song one look. To put just one verse, or the chorus, on a picture of its own (and add extra words like Key change to a slide), do it in the song itself: see A different picture for one verse or the chorus . The rest of the song keeps its theme. Note: Words can’t go over a YouTube video (YouTube doesn’t allow anything on top of its player). Use a video file you have permission to show.","summary":""},{"url":"themes.html#changing-a-theme-or-making-your-own","page":"Themes: how the words look","heading":"Changing a theme, or making your own","text":"Changing a theme, or making your own Click a theme’s picture to change it. Built-in themes can’t be changed: clicking one makes your own copy to change. Or click + New theme . Name . Font , and Largest text size . Long verses get smaller by themselves to fit; this is how big short ones get. Show all fonts on this computer lists every font. Bold , Shadow , ALL CAPITALS . Text colour , Outline (None, Thin, Thick) and its colour. Line up the words (left, centre, right) and Where on the screen (top, middle, bottom). Songs in two languages : for a song with a second language , whether The second language goes Under the first or Line by line , its Size , Colour and Italic . Behind the words : The church background (chosen in the Media tab), A picture or video , A plain colour , or Black . For a video, tick Play the video’s sound to hear it. The Preview shows how song words will look on the projector. Click Save theme . Delete theme removes your own theme; anything using it goes back to Classic. Tip: At the back of the room, bigger and simpler is always easier. White text with a thin outline or shadow on a dark background is easiest of all.","summary":""},{"url":"themes.html#how-slides-change-and-words-appear","page":"Themes: how the words look","heading":"How slides change and words appear","text":"How slides change and words appear At the top of the Themes tab, Changing slides : Fade (the usual), Cut (instant), Slide , Zoom , Rise , and Scroll : the words going off move up and away as the next ones come up from below, like turning a page of a song sheet. Speed: Quick , Normal , Slow . Under it, Words appear : All together : the whole slide at once (the usual). Line by line : each line a moment after the one before. Word by word : each word a moment after the one before, like a lyric video. However long the slide, the last words are there within two seconds, so nobody is left waiting to sing. The speed above sets how quickly the lines or words follow each other. Words appearing one by one look lovely for a slow song, but can be hard to follow for a fast one: try it in a rehearsal first. This is for every screen and every item.","summary":""},{"url":"screens.html","page":"Screens","heading":"","text":"Screens PraiseAV can drive several screens at once, each with its own job.","summary":"Projectors and TVs: which screen shows what, the screen map with tap-to-swap, practice screens for setting up at home, and opening a view in a window."},{"url":"screens.html#the-four-views","page":"Screens","heading":"The four views","text":"The four views View What it shows Usually on Congregation Words, verses, videos, pictures The projector or big TVs at the front Preacher Notes, the band’s words during songs, clock, timer, prompts A TV at the back, facing the platform Timer One big timer and prompts A small screen on the platform Livestream The words in a strip, for a video mixer to lay over the camera A cable to an ATEM or capture card The computer’s own screen is always PraiseAV controls : the window you work in.","summary":""},{"url":"screens.html#plugging-in-screens","page":"Screens","heading":"Plugging in screens","text":"Plugging in screens Plug the projector or TV into the computer (HDMI, usually). Windows must extend onto it, not copy. If Windows is copying the laptop’s screen onto the projector, PraiseAV shows a yellow message at the top: click Use it as a separate screen . (It’s the same as pressing Windows + P and choosing Extend .) PraiseAV finds it. The first screen gets Congregation , the next Preacher , then Timer . PraiseAV remembers each screen’s job. Next Sunday, everything appears in the same place by itself. With no screens plugged in, the congregation view opens in a window, so you can still try everything.","summary":""},{"url":"screens.html#choosing-each-screen-s-job","page":"Screens","heading":"Choosing each screen’s job","text":"Choosing each screen’s job Click Screens… at the top right. Under Each screen’s job , each plugged-in screen has a list: Congregation , Preacher , Timer , Livestream , or Not used .","summary":""},{"url":"screens.html#the-screen-map","page":"Screens","heading":"The screen map","text":"The screen map At the top of the Screens window, a map shows every screen, live, with what’s on it now. Solid boxes with ● Connected are real screens plugged in. Dashed boxes aren’t on a screen yet: Practice only , or Planned: plug in a screen (from your church setup ).","summary":""},{"url":"screens.html#swapping-two-screens","page":"Screens","heading":"Swapping two screens","text":"Swapping two screens The preacher’s notes are on the front projector, and the words on the back TV? Tap one, then tap the other. They swap. Tap a dashed view, then a solid screen, to put that view on it.","summary":""},{"url":"screens.html#the-preacher-screen","page":"Screens","heading":"The preacher screen","text":"The preacher screen What the preacher’s screen shows, and how big, is up to you: see Sermons and the preacher’s screen . During songs it doubles as the band’s words.","summary":""},{"url":"screens.html#practice-screens","page":"Screens","heading":"Practice screens","text":"Practice screens Set everything up at home on a laptop, or show a church what the preacher and timer screens do before they’ve bought any. In Screens… , click Open practice screens on this computer’s screen . One window shows every view side by side, live, as you go through the service. Plan the service and Save it as usual: it s all the real thing, just shown together.","summary":""},{"url":"screens.html#a-monitor-for-watching-every-screen","page":"Screens","heading":"A monitor for watching every screen","text":"A monitor for watching every screen With a monitor plugged in (say the one you use as the preacher s screen), click Open them on screen 2 (or whichever number it has) in Screens… . The practice screens fill that monitor, so you can watch the front, the back wall, the timer and the livestream while you work. That screen s own job pauses while they re open, and comes back when you click Close practice screens . It s handy for demos too: the projector shows the real congregation screen, and everyone can see what the other screens would show on the monitor. Each view has a Show on… list, to put it on a real screen when one is plugged in. Views with no screen say Practice only . Tip: At home, plug in a spare TV or monitor as a pretend projector. Set up the screens, save a church setup , and take it to church on a USB stick or OneDrive.","summary":""},{"url":"screens.html#open-a-view-in-a-window","page":"Screens","heading":"Open a view in a window","text":"Open a view in a window In Screens… , under Open a view in a window , click Congregation , Preacher , Timer or Livestream . It opens in a normal window. This is handy for a screen PraiseAV can’t see by itself (some wireless displays and USB adaptors): drag the window onto it and maximise it.","summary":""},{"url":"screens.html#screens-on-the-network","page":"Screens","heading":"Screens on the network","text":"Screens on the network PraiseAV can also send the words to OBS or vMix, or the congregation screen to a TV in another room, over the church network instead of a cable. In Screens… , scroll down to Show on the network . See Screens on the network .","summary":""},{"url":"screens.html#projectors-and-test-patterns","page":"Screens","heading":"Projectors and test patterns","text":"Projectors and test patterns Turn projectors on and off from PraiseAV, close the shutter, and show test patterns for lining up and focusing: see Projectors and test patterns .","summary":""},{"url":"screens.html#hide-and-show","page":"Screens","heading":"Hide and Show","text":"Hide and Show ⋯ More › Hide the screens , at the top right, takes all the screens away (to use the projector for something else). Show the screens , in the top bar while they re hidden, brings them back.","summary":""},{"url":"screens.html#if-a-screen-isn-t-found","page":"Screens","heading":"If a screen isn’t found","text":"If a screen isn’t found Press Windows + P and choose Extend , not Duplicate. Check the projector is on and set to the right input (HDMI 1, HDMI 2…). Unplug the cable and plug it back in. See Troubleshooting .","summary":""},{"url":"projectors.html","page":"Projectors and test patterns","heading":"","text":"Projectors and test patterns Open Screens… in the top bar and scroll to Projectors and test patterns .","summary":"Turn projectors on and off from PraiseAV over the network (PJLink), close the shutter, see lamp hours and warnings, and show test patterns for lining up and focusing."},{"url":"projectors.html#test-patterns","page":"Projectors and test patterns","heading":"Test patterns","text":"Test patterns For setting up a projector or TV: click one and it shows on every screen. Pattern Use it to check Grid Focus and keystone. The lines should be sharp and square all over, and the circles round. The fine lines in the middle blur first when the focus is off. Colour bars Colour and brightness. Every bar, and every step of the grey scale underneath, should look different. If the darkest steps all look black, turn the brightness up a little; if the lightest all look white, turn the contrast down. White An even picture. Look for dim corners or a colour tint. Edges Whether the edges are cut off. All four yellow arrows and the thin red line round the edge should show. If they don t, the TV is zooming in: look for Just Scan , Screen Fit or 1:1 in its picture settings. Click the pattern again, or Turn off the test pattern , or click any slide, to put the service back. On screen says Test pattern while one is up.","summary":""},{"url":"projectors.html#projectors","page":"Projectors and test patterns","heading":"Projectors","text":"Projectors PraiseAV can turn projectors on and off, and hide the picture, from the computer, over the church s network. Most projectors made in the last fifteen years can be controlled this way. It s called PJLink .","summary":""},{"url":"projectors.html#setting-one-up","page":"Projectors and test patterns","heading":"Setting one up","text":"Setting one up On the projector, open its network settings menu (the remote s Menu button), and: plug it into the church s network with a network cable (or Wi-Fi, if it has it), on the same network as this computer; turn on PJLink (it may be called network control or network standby : turn on standby too, or it can t be turned on over the network); note its IP address , like 192.168.1.50. If it can, fix the address so it doesn t change. In PraiseAV, Screens… › + Add a projector : give it a name ( Main projector ), type its address, and its PJLink password if it has one (some come with a password already set; the manual says what). Save . PraiseAV checks it and says how it is: It s off, lamp used 1,234 hours .","summary":""},{"url":"projectors.html#the-buttons","page":"Projectors and test patterns","heading":"The buttons","text":"The buttons Button What it does Turn on Starts the projector. It takes a minute to warm up ( It s warming up ). Turn off Turns it off, after asking. It cools down for a minute or two, and can t be turned on again until it has. Close shutter Hides the picture (and sound) without turning it off, and it comes back instantly with Open shutter . Better than turning off and on for a short break. Check Asks it again how it is: on or off, the shutter, lamp hours, and any warnings (a filter to clean, too hot, a lamp near the end). Change… Its name, address or password. With two or more projectors there s also Turn them all on and Turn them all off .","summary":""},{"url":"projectors.html#if-it-says-it-can-39-t-talk-to-the-projector","page":"Projectors and test patterns","heading":"If it says it can t talk to the projector","text":"If it says it can t talk to the projector No answer : the projector isn t on the network, or it s switched off at the wall, or network standby is off. Refused the connection : PJLink is turned off in its network settings. The PJLink password is wrong : check the password in its network settings, and click Change… › Change the password . Can t do that just now : it s warming up or cooling down. Wait a minute and try again. The password is kept on this computer only, protected for your Windows account. It isn t in backups or church setup files.","summary":""},{"url":"live-video.html","page":"Live video on the screen","heading":"","text":"Live video on the screen PraiseAV can put live video on the congregation screen with the words over it or under it. For example: A camera on the preacher, so people at the back can see (a big room, or an overflow hall). A YouTube live stream from another church, for a joint service. Open Live video in the right-hand column. (The first time, tick it under + More tools at the bottom of the column.)","summary":"Show a camera, or a YouTube or web stream, on the congregation screen with the words, for overflow rooms, a big room, or a joint service."},{"url":"live-video.html#a-camera","page":"Live video on the screen","heading":"A camera","text":"A camera Plug the camera in. Most cameras need a USB capture device to connect to a computer (see Hardware ). Webcams just plug in. Click Capture device , and choose the camera from the list. Not there? Click Refresh . Choose the sound: usually No sound (the sound desk has it) . Choose how the words show: Words in a strip along the bottom : the picture stays clear. Words in a strip along the top : for a small room, where the people in front hide the bottom of the screen. Use Move the picture down so the words don t cover faces (the floor at the bottom is cut off instead). Words above the picture : the words at the top and the camera underneath, a little smaller, with nothing over it. Words full screen over a dimmed picture . Tick On (or press V ). Songs and verses show with the video. Videos, pictures and the logo still show full screen when you put them on; the camera comes back after.","summary":""},{"url":"live-video.html#phone-cameras","page":"Live video on the screen","heading":"Phone cameras","text":"Phone cameras Old phones make good cameras. Prop one up where it’s needed, on the church Wi-Fi, and its picture comes into PraiseAV. A few donated phones can be your cameras until there’s money for proper ones. In Live video , click Phone camera , then + Add a phone camera . A QR code appears. On the phone, on the same Wi-Fi as the PraiseAV computer, scan the code with its camera. The first time, the phone warns that the connection isn’t private . That’s because PraiseAV is on your computer, not a website on the internet. Tap Advanced (or Show details ), then Continue (or visit this website ). Each phone asks once. Give it a name (“Pulpit camera”), tap Start the camera , and allow the camera. Turn the phone sideways, prop it up, and plug it into a charger . The phone’s picture appears in Live video. Click it to put it on screen. The phone says ● LIVE on screen while PraiseAV is showing it. Add as many phones as you like. Each phone remembers its name, so next time just open the page again and tap Start the camera . Tip: Phone cameras send up to 20 pictures a second over the Wi-Fi, slightly behind real life. That’s fine for a camera on the screen or a recording , but for lip-sync-perfect livestreams, cameras plugged into a capture card are better. On the phone, ⛶ Full screen helps you line up the shot, and 🌙 Dark screen keeps sending with the screen black to save battery.","summary":""},{"url":"live-video.html#choosing-between-cameras","page":"Live video on the screen","heading":"Choosing between cameras","text":"Choosing between cameras With more than one camera, Live video shows every camera’s live picture side by side : phones and cameras plugged into the computer together. Click a picture to cut to it. The one on screen has a red border and LIVE . For a bigger view, click ⛶ Bigger : all the cameras fill this computer’s screen, so whoever is choosing the shots can see them clearly. Click one to cut to it there too, and close it when you’re done. A phone that stops sending (flat battery, off the Wi-Fi) shows Not connected and can’t be chosen. Its ✕ takes it off the list. Tip: Each picture costs this computer a little, so 3 or 4 cameras is comfortable. The camera on screen is never affected. Sync… beside the camera pictures keeps a recording s sound in step with each camera. See Recording: sound and picture in step . The first time phone cameras are switched on, Windows may ask on the PraiseAV computer whether to allow PraiseAV on private networks : choose Allow .","summary":""},{"url":"live-video.html#youtube-or-a-web-link","page":"Live video on the screen","heading":"YouTube or a web link","text":"YouTube or a web link Click YouTube or web link . Paste the link (for example https://youtube.com/live/… ) and click Use . Tick On . The video sits at the top with the words underneath: YouTube doesn’t allow anything on top of its player. Note: This needs good internet at church. Streams run a few seconds behind.","summary":""},{"url":"live-video.html#the-logo-in-the-corner","page":"Live video on the screen","heading":"The logo in the corner","text":"The logo in the corner Tick Church logo in the corner and choose the corner. This works with or without live video. Choose your logo in the Media tab first, or a different picture just for the corner (see Media ).","summary":""},{"url":"live-video.html#recording-instead","page":"Live video on the screen","heading":"Recording instead","text":"Recording instead No livestream? Record the service : with Live video on, the recording shows the camera with the words.","summary":""},{"url":"live-video.html#livestreaming-is-different","page":"Live video on the screen","heading":"Livestreaming is different","text":"Livestreaming is different This page is about showing video in the room . To send your service out to YouTube or Facebook, see Livestream and ATEM .","summary":""},{"url":"live-captions.html","page":"Live captions","heading":"","text":"Live captions Live captions turn speech from a microphone or a video playing on the congregation screen into words along the bottom of that screen: the sermon, the notices, the prayers. They help people who are hard of hearing, and anyone sitting where the sound is poor. The speech is recognised on this computer . Nothing that s said is sent anywhere or kept. Live captions currently recognise English speech only .","summary":"What the preacher says, as words along the bottom of the congregation screen, for people who are hard of hearing. Recognised on this computer, without the internet."},{"url":"live-captions.html#turning-them-on","page":"Live captions","heading":"Turning them on","text":"Turning them on In the right-hand column, open Live captions . (The first time, tick it under + More tools at the bottom of the column.) Under Caption accuracy , choose Fast for words as they are spoken, or More accurate for steadier words several seconds later. Under Sound from , choose Microphone or sound desk (see below) or Video playing on congregation screen . Tick On . Each mode downloads its speech model once: about 100 MB from GitHub for Fast, or about 375 MB from Hugging Face for More accurate. A bar shows progress. If the church’s web filter blocks the download, allow the relevant site or turn captions on at home first. After the model is downloaded, recognising a local preacher works without the internet. A web stream still needs its own connection. When it says Listening , speak and the words appear on the screen and in the panel. Fast shows changing words while someone is talking. More accurate waits for a short piece of speech before showing it: in one test on this computer, its first words appeared 7.8 seconds after speech began. Fast showed a provisional fragment after 1.2 seconds in the same test. The delay can differ on another computer. The words clear after a few seconds of quiet. Turn captions off to change modes.","summary":""},{"url":"live-captions.html#the-best-sound","page":"Live captions","heading":"The best sound","text":"The best sound Captions are only as good as the sound they hear. Best: a cable from the sound desk (a spare aux or record output) into the computer: into its microphone socket, or a USB sound adaptor, which costs a few pounds. The words come out clean, without the room s echo and the congregation s coughs. All right: a USB microphone near the preacher, or on the lectern. Not good: the laptop s own microphone at the back of the room. It hears the whole room.","summary":""},{"url":"live-captions.html#another-church-s-stream","page":"Live captions","heading":"Another church’s stream","text":"Another church’s stream Add the parent church’s YouTube or Vimeo link to the service and put its video on the congregation screen. Under Sound from , choose Video playing on congregation screen , then turn captions on. PraiseAV listens directly to that screen’s sound, without a cable or a room microphone. The video remains audible. Captions appear in a separate strip below the player, so they do not cover it. Test the actual stream before the service. Internet is needed for the video, even after the caption model has been downloaded. Advertisements and any other sound in the video may also be captioned. If the stream moves from speech to a hymn or other music, turn captions Off and turn them back On when speaking resumes; sung words can come out garbled. For a local preacher, switch Sound from back to Microphone or sound desk .","summary":""},{"url":"live-captions.html#songs","page":"Live captions","heading":"Songs","text":"Songs While a PraiseAV song item is on the screen, captions pause: the song s words are already there, and singing doesn t caption well. This automatic pause does not detect songs inside a web stream. To caption during PraiseAV songs too (say, for a solo whose words aren t on screen), tick Show during songs too .","summary":""},{"url":"live-captions.html#size-and-place","page":"Live captions","heading":"Size and place","text":"Size and place Choose Small , Normal or Large . The captions sit in a dark strip along the bottom of the congregation screen, above any parent alerts, and the slide s words move up to make room.","summary":""},{"url":"live-captions.html#what-to-expect","page":"Live captions","heading":"What to expect","text":"What to expect Both modes can mishear names, Bible places, unusual words, and overlapping speakers. More accurate reduced mistakes in our recorded sermon and audiobook tests, but it does not promise a verbatim transcript. Rehearse with the actual sound desk and preacher before relying on it during a service. More accurate uses more processing power. If its words fall further and further behind on the church computer, switch to Fast or use a stronger computer.","summary":""},{"url":"live-captions.html#credits","page":"Live captions","heading":"Credits","text":"Credits Fast uses NVIDIA s NeMo streaming FastConformer English model (CC BY 4.0). More accurate uses OpenAI s Whisper small English model (MIT), converted to ONNX by the sherpa-onnx project. Both run with the free sherpa-onnx toolkit (Apache 2.0).","summary":""},{"url":"go-live.html","page":"Going live on YouTube","heading":"","text":"Going live on YouTube PraiseAV can livestream the service itself: the congregation screen (the camera with the words, or the slides) and the sound desk s sound, straight to YouTube, with no other program to learn. It lets a church start streaming before it’s ready to invest in a video switcher; when it is, PraiseAV works with an ATEM Mini too.","summary":"Livestream the service straight to YouTube (or Facebook) from PraiseAV with your stream key: start streaming without an ATEM or OBS, and add them as your church grows."},{"url":"go-live.html#once-the-stream-key","page":"Going live on YouTube","heading":"Once: the stream key","text":"Once: the stream key In YouTube Studio , click Create › Go live , then Stream . The very first time, YouTube may ask you to confirm your channel, and it can take up to a day before live streaming is switched on. Copy the Stream key with the copy button beside it, in the main (left) panel . If YouTube shows a second, Vertical key on the right (its Dual stream for Shorts), don t use that one: the stream would go to the vertical version and the main one would say No data . In PraiseAV, click ◉ Go live at the top, paste the key into Stream key , and click Save . The key is kept on this computer, protected by Windows, and is only ever sent to YouTube. Keep it private: anyone with it can stream to your channel. If it ever gets out, click Reset beside it in YouTube Studio and paste the new one into PraiseAV.","summary":""},{"url":"go-live.html#finding-your-way-round-youtube-studio","page":"Going live on YouTube","heading":"Finding your way round YouTube Studio","text":"Finding your way round YouTube Studio YouTube uses a few words of its own, and moves things round now and then: Encoder (or streaming software ) means the program sending the video: that s PraiseAV . Nothing to install. If YouTube asks you to choose one, pick Other , or leave it as it is. Stream key : under the preview, beside Key: . Use the copy button (two squares) beside it. A key you keep using each week (the default one, or one you ve named) is best. Who can see it (Visibility, or Privacy) : click Edit beside the stream s title at the top of the page (sometimes a pencil, or in the ⋮ menu), then choose Private (only you), Unlisted (only people with the link) or Public . For a first try, choose Private . Can t find Edit ? In Create › Go live , choose Schedule stream instead: its steps ask for the visibility as you go, and the broadcast waits for PraiseAV on Sunday. No data next to the preview just means PraiseAV isn t sending yet. It changes a few seconds after you click ● Go live now , then Preparing stream for up to half a minute, then the picture. Dual stream (under Stream settings : a vertical version for Shorts) can stay on. PraiseAV sends YouTube exactly the picture shape it expects, so both versions work. (Older versions of PraiseAV could sit on Preparing stream with it on.) Each time can be a new broadcast. When the last one has finished, YouTube starts a new one for the next stream, with its own settings: check Visibility each time, or set it up once with Schedule stream for Sunday. Which key is in use : the Key: box above the preview chooses which of your keys this broadcast listens to. It must be the same key that s in PraiseAV: if YouTube says No data while PraiseAV says ● LIVE , choose your other key there (for example Default stream key ). Visibility is also shown in Content › Live . Check it there too: it s the one YouTube actually uses. Tip: In YouTube Studio s stream settings, switch on Auto-start and Auto-stop . Then the broadcast starts by itself when PraiseAV goes live, and ends when it stops, with no need to touch YouTube on Sunday.","summary":""},{"url":"go-live.html#on-sunday","page":"Going live on YouTube","heading":"On Sunday","text":"On Sunday Click ◉ Go live . Check Sound from is the sound desk s input , and the meter moves when someone speaks or sings. Choose the picture quality to suit the church s internet: Best: 1080p , the usual choice, and the best for people watching it back later (about 8 Mbps upload: most fibre broadband has that). Good: 720p if the button often says · slow internet at 1080p (about 4 Mbps upload). Slow internet: 480p if it still struggles (about 2 Mbps upload). Not sure? Start at 1080p. If the internet couldn t keep up, PraiseAV says so when the stream ends, and suggests the next one down. Leave Also save a recording on this computer ticked, for a copy to keep (and to upload again if the stream had trouble). That copy is always full 1080p , even when the stream is smaller. It s saved in the recordings folder shown below the tick (see Where recordings go ). Click ● Go live now . The button at the top turns red: ● LIVE and how long you ve been live. What goes out is about 2 seconds behind the screen (PraiseAV sends it at a steady pace, as YouTube likes), and YouTube adds its own few seconds before viewers see it. Run the service as normal: whatever s on the congregation screen goes out, with the camera and words if Live video is on, or the slides if not. Congregation alerts never go out. A parent called from the crèche, or a scrolling notice, shows in the room but not on the livestream (or in its recording), so children s numbers stay private. See Congregation alerts . To finish, click ● LIVE , then ■ Stop the livestream in the question below it ( Keep going if you clicked by mistake; the stream carries on while it asks). If Also save a recording was ticked, the recording s message has Copy to USB stick .","summary":""},{"url":"go-live.html#if-the-internet-struggles","page":"Going live on YouTube","heading":"If the internet struggles","text":"If the internet struggles · slow internet on the button: the internet can t keep up, so a few pictures are being skipped to stay up to date. If it happens a lot, use a lower picture quality next time, or plug the computer into the router with a network cable (much better than Wi-Fi). · reconnecting… : the connection dropped. PraiseAV keeps trying by itself and carries on the same broadcast when it s back. You don t need to do anything.","summary":""},{"url":"go-live.html#sound-and-picture-in-step","page":"Going live on YouTube","heading":"Sound and picture in step","text":"Sound and picture in step The sound is held back to match whichever camera is on screen, as for recordings. Measure each camera once: see Sound and picture in step .","summary":""},{"url":"go-live.html#facebook-and-others","page":"Going live on YouTube","heading":"Facebook and others","text":"Facebook and others In Stream to , choose Facebook and paste the stream key from Facebook s Live Producer . For another service, choose Another service and paste its secure RTMPS address (it starts with rtmps:// ) and its stream key. PraiseAV rejects an unencrypted rtmp:// address for a remote service. Licences: livestreaming songs needs a CCLI Streaming Licence (or your country s equivalent), and permission for any videos you show.","summary":""},{"url":"go-live.html#with-an-atem-or-obs","page":"Going live on YouTube","heading":"With an ATEM, or OBS","text":"With an ATEM, or OBS You don’t need them to start. When your church is ready for an ATEM Mini, or already uses OBS, PraiseAV works with both: see Livestream and ATEM .","summary":""},{"url":"recording.html","page":"Recording the service","heading":"","text":"Recording the service If your church can’t livestream (no internet, or nobody to run it), PraiseAV can record the service to a video file. Upload it to YouTube or Facebook afterwards, or share it with people who couldn’t come. It records what’s on the congregation screen : the slides, or with Live video on, the camera with the words over it. Congregation alerts are left out : a child’s number called from the crèche, or a scrolling notice, shows in the room but never in the recording (see Congregation alerts ).","summary":"No livestream? Record what's on the congregation screen, with the sound desk's sound, to a video file to upload to YouTube or Facebook later."},{"url":"recording.html#recording","page":"Recording the service","heading":"Recording","text":"Recording Click ● Record at the top of PraiseAV. Choose where the sound comes from: The sound desk : the best. Run a lead from the desk into the computer, usually through a small USB audio interface (see Hardware ), and choose it here. This computer’s sound : only PraiseAV’s own videos and music, not the people speaking or singing. A microphone : works, but a laptop’s microphone hears the room and sounds echoey. With an input chosen, a sound meter moves when sound comes in, and says ✓ Sound is coming in . If it stays still, check the lead and that the desk is sending to it. With No sound , PraiseAV warns that the recording will be silent. Click ● Start recording . The laptop s headphone socket instead of a USB box? It can work for a first try. Most laptops have one socket for a headset: you need a cheap headset splitter to get a separate microphone plug. The desk s output is much louder than a microphone, so turn its send right down (or use an attenuator lead , −20 to −30 dB), or it crackles. It s mono, and can hum with the charger plugged in. A small USB audio box (about £25) avoids all of that, so it s the better choice for every week. The button shows ■ REC and the time while it records. Click it, then ■ Stop and save in the question below it. (It keeps recording while it asks.) The file is named with the date and the service. Click Show the file in the message to open the folder.","summary":""},{"url":"recording.html#where-recordings-go","page":"Recording the service","heading":"Where recordings go","text":"Where recordings go Recordings are saved in Documents › PraiseAV Recordings unless you choose another folder. An hour takes about 3 GB, so a small laptop drive fills up in a few months. The Record window shows where they re going and how much room is left (for example room for about 40 hours ). Change… chooses another folder: a bigger drive, or an external hard drive or large USB drive kept plugged in for recordings. Use Documents again goes back. Nearly full? The Record window warns when there s room for less than about two hours. Copy old recordings off (or delete ones already uploaded to YouTube), or choose a bigger drive. The external drive isn t plugged in? The Record window says so, and the service is still recorded, in Documents › PraiseAV Recordings , so nothing is missed. Copy it across later. Full, or unplugged while recording? The REC button says not saving! and a message says what happened. What was recorded until then is kept. The folder is remembered on this computer only, because drive letters differ between computers. Recordings on this computer , in the Record window, lists the latest from both places.","summary":""},{"url":"recording.html#sound-and-picture-in-step","page":"Recording the service","heading":"Sound and picture in step","text":"Sound and picture in step A camera s picture reaches PraiseAV a little after the sound desk s sound: a USB webcam about a tenth of a second, a phone over the Wi-Fi up to half a second. In a recording, lips would move after the words. So PraiseAV holds the sound back by the right amount for whichever camera is on screen, and changes it when you cut to another camera. Measure each camera once. Click Sync… in Live video (or Sound and picture in step… in Record): Phones measure themselves. The phone lines its clock up with the computer s, and each picture says when it was taken. Click Use this beside the measured time. Test sync (for any camera that can see the projector screen): the screen flashes white three times while the computer beeps. The beep needs to reach the sound desk, or the room speakers so the microphones hear it. PraiseAV finds the flashes and beeps in a short test clip, measures the gap, and saves it. Clap test (for a camera that can t see the screen): clap three times where the camera can see your hands. Play it back and slide until each clap s sound lands as the hands meet. Then save. It s remembered on this computer, so it only needs doing again if a camera changes. One lead into one side? If the desk only has one output to spare, plugged into input 1, only the left side would have sound. PraiseAV notices and puts it on both sides; the REC button says mono . If the sound input is unplugged while recording, the button says sound lost! , and the picture keeps recording.","summary":""},{"url":"recording.html#copying-it-to-a-usb-stick","page":"Recording the service","heading":"Copying it to a USB stick","text":"Copying it to a USB stick The recording is always saved on this computer first . Then copy it to a USB stick, so if the stick fails or is pulled out, nothing is lost. Plug in the USB stick. In the message after recording, click Copy to USB stick . (Or later: ● Record lists the last few recordings, each with Copy to USB stick .) Wait for ✓ Copied … and checked. Safe to take the stick out. PraiseAV reads the copy back from the stick to make sure it s complete. It goes in a PraiseAV Recordings folder on the stick. If the stick is too full, PraiseAV says how much space it needs. Tip: Turn Live video on with a phone camera pointed at the front, and the recording shows the room with the song words along the bottom, like a livestream.","summary":""},{"url":"recording.html#good-to-know","page":"Recording the service","heading":"Good to know","text":"Good to know The recording is an MP4 file at the projector’s size (usually 1920 × 1080), which YouTube and Facebook accept as it is. It’s saved a few seconds at a time, so a power cut loses only the last few seconds. If the congregation screen closes (the projector is unplugged, or the screens are hidden with Hide the screens ), the recording stops and is saved. The laptop doesn’t sleep while it’s recording. Plug it in: an hour’s recording uses a lot of battery. An hour is about 2.5 GB. Make sure there’s space on the computer. Note: Putting a service online needs the right licences: a CCLI Streaming Licence for the songs, and permission for any videos you show. Recording for your own use needs no streaming licence until you upload it.","summary":""},{"url":"recording.html#with-an-atem-mini","page":"Recording the service","heading":"With an ATEM Mini","text":"With an ATEM Mini If you have an ATEM Mini Pro, it can record too ( ● Record next to Go live ): see Livestream and ATEM .","summary":""},{"url":"livestream-and-atem.html","page":"Livestream and ATEM","heading":"","text":"Livestream and ATEM No ATEM yet? PraiseAV can stream to YouTube by itself, with the camera and words together, so you can start before your church is ready to invest in one: see Going live on YouTube . This page is for when you have an ATEM or use OBS. When you stream a service, viewers at home want to see the room and read the words. The Livestream screen gives a video mixer just the words, in a strip, ready to lay over the camera.","summary":"Put the song words over your camera on a livestream, with a Blackmagic ATEM Mini or OBS. Start and stop streaming and recording from PraiseAV."},{"url":"livestream-and-atem.html#how-it-fits-together","page":"Livestream and ATEM","heading":"How it fits together","text":"How it fits together Camera HDMI ATEM Mini Pro input 1 PraiseAV computer Livestream screen HDMI ATEM Mini Pro input 2 Sound desk a separate mix for the stream 3.5 mm audio ATEM Mini Pro mic input ATEM Mini Pro Ethernet to the router YouTube or Facebook and PraiseAV controls it The ATEM keys the words over the camera: it cuts out the black (or green) and keeps the words. See Example setups for the whole picture.","summary":""},{"url":"livestream-and-atem.html#setting-up-the-livestream-screen","page":"Livestream and ATEM","heading":"Setting up the Livestream screen","text":"Setting up the Livestream screen Plug a second HDMI output of the computer into an ATEM input. In Screens… , give that screen the Livestream job. Under Livestream in the same window, choose the Background : Black: for a luma key : the usual for an ATEM. Green: for a chroma key (the words sit on a dark band): for OBS, vMix or mixers that key on green. Optional: your CCLI Streaming Licence number . It shows small under the song details. Only songs and verses show on the Livestream screen. Congregation alerts never go on it , so children’s numbers stay private.","summary":""},{"url":"livestream-and-atem.html#controlling-an-atem-from-praiseav","page":"Livestream and ATEM","heading":"Controlling an ATEM from PraiseAV","text":"Controlling an ATEM from PraiseAV PraiseAV can talk to a Blackmagic ATEM Mini (any model) over the network. Plug the ATEM into the same network as the computer with an Ethernet cable (USB alone isn’t enough). Click ATEM… at the top of PraiseAV, and tick Control a Blackmagic ATEM switcher from PraiseAV . Type the ATEM’s address and click Connect . A new ATEM Mini uses 192.168.10.240 . Blackmagic’s ATEM Setup app shows it. When it says Connected , choose: Which input is this computer (the slides)? Keyer that puts the livestream words over the camera : usually Downstream key 1 . Or None if you’d rather do it on the ATEM’s buttons. Tick Turn the words on and off by themselves so the words appear when a song or verse is on screen and go away for the sermon. Under Try it : Show slides , Back to camera , Words on , Words off . Check each one works. The window also shows the ATEM’s model and firmware. If the firmware is older than 8.0, update it with ATEM Setup.","summary":""},{"url":"livestream-and-atem.html#during-the-service","page":"Livestream and ATEM","heading":"During the service","text":"During the service At the top of PraiseAV: ● Go live starts streaming, ● Record starts recording (on models that can). They show ON AIR and REC with the time. PraiseAV asks before starting or stopping the stream. In the right-hand column, Livestream shows what’s on the stream, with Show slides and Back to camera for full-screen slides (a video, notices). The stream key and YouTube scheduling stay in ATEM Software Control and YouTube Studio : set them up once there. If something isn’t working, Open the ATEM log (bottom of the ATEM window) keeps a record to send to whoever helps you.","summary":""},{"url":"livestream-and-atem.html#with-obs-or-vmix-instead","page":"Livestream and ATEM","heading":"With OBS or vMix instead","text":"With OBS or vMix instead OBS Studio is free streaming software for a computer; vMix is a paid one. The easiest way needs no cables: turn on Screens… › Show on the network and add the words page to OBS as a Browser source (or to vMix as a Web Browser input). See Screens on the network . Or, with a cable: Use a capture card to bring the Livestream screen into the OBS computer (or run OBS on the PraiseAV computer and use a Window capture of a Livestream window: Screens… › Open a view in a window › Livestream ). Choose the Green background. In OBS, add a Chroma Key filter to that source and put it above the camera.","summary":""},{"url":"screens-on-the-network.html","page":"Screens on the network","heading":"","text":"Screens on the network PraiseAV can show what it shows as a web page on the church’s own network. Two pages: Page What it shows For Words for OBS or vMix Just the words (and the song details), on a see-through background Laying the words over the cameras on a livestream Congregation screen The words, verses, pictures and backgrounds, full screen A TV or computer in another room: an overflow hall, the foyer, the parents’ room Nothing to install, no extra cables and no licence. Any computer, laptop, tablet or smart TV on the same network can open them. Tip: People in the congregation can also follow the words on their own phone, in big letters. See Words on phones . Congregation alerts never go on these pages. Children’s numbers and notices are for the main room only, so they never reach the stream or the other rooms. The preacher’s notes and prompts don’t go out either.","summary":"Put the words into OBS or vMix, or show the congregation screen on a TV in another room, through the church network. No cables, no NDI, no licence."},{"url":"screens-on-the-network.html#turning-it-on","page":"Screens on the network","heading":"Turning it on","text":"Turning it on Click Screens… at the top right. Scroll down to Show on the network and click Turn on . The first time, Windows may ask whether to allow PraiseAV on the network. Tick Private networks and click Allow . PraiseAV shows the addresses to use, with a Copy button next to each. It stays on next time you open PraiseAV, until you click Turn off .","summary":""},{"url":"screens-on-the-network.html#words-in-obs","page":"Screens on the network","heading":"Words in OBS","text":"Words in OBS In Screens… › Show on the network , choose Where the words go (a strip along the bottom, a strip along the top, or full screen) and the Background (see-through is best for OBS). Click Copy next to the address: OBS or vMix on this computer : when OBS runs on the PraiseAV computer. On another computer : when OBS runs on a different computer. In OBS, under Sources , click + and choose Browser . Give it a name, such as Words , and click OK . Paste the address into URL . Set Width to 1920 and Height to 1080 . Click OK . In the Sources list, drag Words above your camera. The words appear over the camera on a dark band and change the moment you change the slide. Black , Clear text and Logo take them off the stream. Tip: If the words look blurry, right-click the Words source, choose Transform › Fit to screen , and check the Width and Height are 1920 and 1080.","summary":""},{"url":"screens-on-the-network.html#words-in-vmix","page":"Screens on the network","heading":"Words in vMix","text":"Words in vMix Copy the address, as for OBS. In vMix, click Add Input and choose Web Browser . Paste the address, set the size to 1920 × 1080 , and click OK . Put the input on an Overlay channel over the cameras (click the 1 under the input to show it as Overlay 1).","summary":""},{"url":"screens-on-the-network.html#other-choices-in-the-address","page":"Screens on the network","heading":"Other choices in the address","text":"Other choices in the address The address can say where the words go and what’s behind them. PraiseAV builds it for you when you choose in the Screens window, but you can also type it: Add to the address What it does ?place=top A strip along the top ?place=full The words big, in the middle of the screen ?bg=green A green background, for programs that key out green ?bg=black A black background For example: http://192.168.1.20:8260/words?place=top bg=green . Without any of them the words are in a strip along the bottom, see-through.","summary":""},{"url":"screens-on-the-network.html#a-screen-in-another-room","page":"Screens on the network","heading":"A screen in another room","text":"A screen in another room For an overflow hall, the foyer TV, a parents’ room or a crèche. In Screens… › Show on the network , find A screen in another room . On the TV, tablet or laptop in the other room, open the web browser and type the address shown (it ends in /screen ). On a tablet you can scan the QR code with the camera instead. Click or tap the page once to make it full screen . It shows what the congregation screen shows: the words, verses, pictures, backgrounds and the logo, and goes black when you click Black . Camera pictures (live video) and YouTube videos don’t go to the other room; there it shows the words over the background instead. The other room has no sound from PraiseAV: run a speaker cable or use the sound desk for that. Tip: An old smart TV’s browser may not show the page. A cheap streaming stick with a web browser (such as a Fire TV Stick with the Silk browser), or an old laptop plugged into the TV, works well.","summary":""},{"url":"screens-on-the-network.html#if-it-doesn-t-work","page":"Screens on the network","heading":"If it doesn’t work","text":"If it doesn’t work Same network : the other computer or TV must be on the same network (the same Wi-Fi) as the PraiseAV computer. Guest Wi-Fi often keeps devices apart: use the staff Wi-Fi or a network cable. The firewall : if Windows asked about the firewall and someone clicked Cancel , other devices can’t reach PraiseAV. Search the Start menu for Allow an app through Windows Firewall , find PraiseAV , and tick Private . (Ask whoever looks after the computer if you’re not sure.) The address changed : if the PraiseAV computer joined a different network, its address changes. Copy the new one from Screens… . OBS on the same computer can use the on this computer address, which never changes. Black in OBS where the camera should be : the Background is set to Black . Choose See-through and copy the address again. The other room says “Can’t reach PraiseAV” : PraiseAV was closed or the pages were turned off. It carries on by itself when PraiseAV is back. Note: These pages only work inside your building’s network. They aren’t on the internet, and they can only show: nobody can change anything from them.","summary":""},{"url":"words-on-phones.html","page":"Words on phones","heading":"","text":"Words on phones Some people find the screen hard to read: their eyesight isn’t what it was, they’re hard of hearing and like to read along, or they sit behind a pillar. With Words on phones they can follow the words on their own phone , as big as they like. It shows the song’s words , the Bible reading and the sermon slides , and moves on by itself when you change the slide. For a song it shows the whole verse or chorus, with the lines on the screen now marked. For a Bible reading it shows the whole reading, with the verses on the screen now marked, and the name of the translation. Nothing to install. People scan a code with their phone’s camera and a web page opens. No PIN, and nobody can change anything from it. The page only shows the words. It only works inside the church , on the church Wi-Fi. Nothing goes on the internet. Some things never go to the phones: parent alerts and notices, the preacher’s notes and prompts, and the band’s chords. Only the words.","summary":"People in the congregation can follow the words on their own phone, in big letters. Nothing to install, no PIN, and it only works inside the church."},{"url":"words-on-phones.html#turning-it-on","page":"Words on phones","heading":"Turning it on","text":"Turning it on Click Screens… at the top right. Scroll down to Words on phones and click Turn on . The first time, Windows may ask whether to allow PraiseAV on the network. Tick Private networks and click Allow . PraiseAV shows a QR code and the address. It stays on next time you open PraiseAV, until you click Turn off .","summary":""},{"url":"words-on-phones.html#putting-the-code-on-the-screen","page":"Words on phones","heading":"Putting the code on the screen","text":"Putting the code on the screen Before the service, put a slide on the screen that tells people how to join. In Screens… › Words on phones , click Add the “Words on your phone” slide to the service . It goes at the top of the service. Click Done , then show the slide like any other, before the service starts. The slide says Words on your phone , with a big QR code and the address under it. The code always has the right address, even if the computer’s address changes from one week to the next. You can change the words on the slide, or move the code, in the slide designer : select the item and click Edit slides . Keep the code large, so phones at the back can read it. Tip: Put the slide in your announcements before the service, and again during the notices, for latecomers.","summary":""},{"url":"words-on-phones.html#what-people-do-on-their-phone","page":"Words on phones","heading":"What people do on their phone","text":"What people do on their phone Join the church Wi-Fi . Open the camera and point it at the code on the screen. Tap the link that appears. (Or open the web browser and type the address shown under the code, such as 192.168.1.20:8270 .) The words appear, and follow the screen by themselves. At the top of the page there are three buttons: Button What it does A− Smaller words A+ Bigger words ◐ Light or dark background The phone remembers the size and background for next time. Before the service the page says Waiting for the service to start . When there are no words on the screen (a picture, a video, or Black or Logo ), it says so, and the words come back by themselves. Tip: Most phones keep the screen on while the page is open, once you’ve tapped it. If a phone still goes dark, tap it to wake it up: the page carries on where the service is.","summary":""},{"url":"words-on-phones.html#how-many-are-following","page":"Words on phones","heading":"How many are following","text":"How many are following Screens… › Words on phones shows how many phones are following now.","summary":""},{"url":"words-on-phones.html#if-it-doesn-t-work","page":"Words on phones","heading":"If it doesn’t work","text":"If it doesn’t work The church Wi-Fi : the phone must be on the same Wi-Fi as the PraiseAV computer. A guest Wi-Fi often keeps phones apart from the computer, and then the page won’t open. Ask whoever looks after the Wi-Fi, or put the PraiseAV computer on the guest Wi-Fi. Mobile data : a phone using mobile data (4G or 5G) instead of the Wi-Fi can’t reach the page. Turn the Wi-Fi on. The firewall : if Windows asked about the firewall and someone clicked Cancel , phones can’t reach PraiseAV. Search the Start menu for Allow an app through Windows Firewall , find PraiseAV , and tick Private . (Ask whoever looks after the computer if you’re not sure.) The phone says “Can’t reach the church computer” : PraiseAV was closed, or Words on phones was turned off. It carries on by itself when PraiseAV is back. An old address : if someone saved the page last week and the computer’s address has changed, scan the code again. Note: Words on phones only works inside your building’s network. It isn’t on the internet, and it only shows the words: nobody can change anything from it.","summary":""},{"url":"phone-remote.html","page":"Phone and tablet remote","heading":"","text":"Phone and tablet remote Anyone you allow can control PraiseAV from a phone, tablet or laptop, in its web browser. Nothing to install, and it works on Android, iPhone, iPad and laptops. An AV remote does what the desk does: Next, Black, Logo, any slide in the service. A preacher remote moves the preacher’s own slides and notes, and shows the timer.","summary":"Control PraiseAV from a phone, tablet or laptop on the church Wi-Fi: AV remote or preacher remote. No app to install."},{"url":"phone-remote.html#switching-it-on","page":"Phone and tablet remote","heading":"Switching it on","text":"Switching it on Click Remote at the top right of PraiseAV. Click Switch remote control on .","summary":""},{"url":"phone-remote.html#connecting-a-phone","page":"Phone and tablet remote","heading":"Connecting a phone","text":"Connecting a phone The phone must be on the same Wi-Fi as the PraiseAV computer. Scan the code with the phone’s camera. Or type the https:// address shown into the phone’s browser, then the PIN . The first time, Android or iPhone may warn that this local certificate is not recognised: open the details and choose to visit the site. Type a name (“Pastor Sam”) and tap Connect . On the PraiseAV computer, a message asks about it. Click Allow as AV remote , Allow as preacher remote , or No . Nobody can control PraiseAV until you allow them at the desk. The PIN changes each time remote control is switched on. Allowed devices are listed under Allowed devices . Next time, they connect straight away. Remove stops one. After updating from an older HTTP version of PraiseAV, pair each phone again once so its old connection key is replaced.","summary":""},{"url":"phone-remote.html#the-av-remote","page":"Phone and tablet remote","heading":"The AV remote","text":"The AV remote ◀ Back and Next ▶ , big. Black , Clear text , Logo . What’s on screen now. The Service : tap any item, then any slide. For sermons: ◀ Notes and Notes ▶ .","summary":""},{"url":"phone-remote.html#the-preacher-remote","page":"Phone and tablet remote","heading":"The preacher remote","text":"The preacher remote ▶ Start my sermon : puts the sermon on screen, for when there’s no one at the laptop. It shows whenever the sermon isn’t on screen. ◀ Slide and Slide ▶ for the congregation slides (while the sermon is on screen). ◀ Notes and Notes ▶ , with the notes themselves on the phone or laptop. The timer , which turns amber and then red, like the preacher’s screen. A preacher with a laptop on the lectern can use it as a notes screen and a clicker together. No one free for the laptop? See Running a service without an AV volunteer .","summary":""},{"url":"phone-remote.html#leading-worship-and-preaching-from-one-phone","page":"Phone and tablet remote","heading":"Leading worship and preaching from one phone","text":"Leading worship and preaching from one phone Someone who leads worship and preaches (a worship leader who is also the youth pastor, say) only needs one phone, allowed once as an AV remote : At the bottom of the AV remote, tap ⇄ Switch to the preacher’s view for the sermon: the notes, the timer, Start my sermon and the slide buttons. Tap ⇄ Switch to the AV view to lead worship again and finish the service. Switch as often as you like. There’s no pairing again, and nobody needs to go to the laptop. A preacher remote can’t switch, so a visiting speaker can’t change the songs. To give someone more, change their phone at the laptop: Remote → Allowed devices , choose AV remote (and preacher’s view) next to their name. There’s no need to pair again. Forget this phone (or Switch to a different remote on a preacher remote) takes the phone off the list; it then has to join again.","summary":""},{"url":"phone-remote.html#if-the-laptop-s-battery-is-low","page":"Phone and tablet remote","heading":"If the laptop’s battery is low","text":"If the laptop’s battery is low The remotes show Laptop battery 25%: plug it in at the top when the laptop is running low on battery.","summary":""},{"url":"phone-remote.html#phones-can-t-connect","page":"Phone and tablet remote","heading":"Phones can’t connect?","text":"Phones can’t connect? Same network : guest Wi-Fi often keeps devices apart. Use the staff Wi-Fi. The first time , Windows asks on the PraiseAV computer whether to allow PraiseAV on private networks : choose Allow . Wi-Fi with no internet : the phone may switch to mobile data. On Android, tap Stay connected when asked. On iPhone, turn mobile data off while using the remote. A computer with no Wi-Fi (most desktops): plug it into the church’s Wi-Fi router with a network cable . The cable and that router’s Wi-Fi are one network, so phones on the Wi-Fi can reach it. No Wi-Fi at all? A cheap Wi-Fi router (about £20–30) works with no internet line: plug the PraiseAV computer into it with a cable and join the phones to its Wi-Fi. Windows’ Mobile hotspot (Settings › Network internet) may work on a laptop too, but Windows often won’t start it unless the laptop is already on a network, so try it before relying on it. See Wi-Fi for phones . Note: The remote only works inside your building’s network. It isn’t on the internet, and PraiseAV doesn’t send anything anywhere.","summary":""},{"url":"example-setups.html","page":"Example setups","heading":"","text":"Example setups Every church is different, but most fit one of these. Start with the simplest that does what you need. You can always add to it later, and practice screens let you try a bigger setup before buying anything. For what to buy, see Hardware . Tip: Count the screens that show different things. Each one needs its own output from the computer. Two screens showing the same thing can share one output with a splitter.","summary":"What a PraiseAV setup looks like in practice: from one laptop and a projector, to back-wall TVs, cameras, overflow rooms and livestreaming with an ATEM Mini or OBS."},{"url":"example-setups.html#1-one-laptop-and-a-projector","page":"Example setups","heading":"1. One laptop and a projector","text":"1. One laptop and a projector The simplest setup, and where most churches start. Laptop PraiseAV controls HDMI Projector or big TV Congregation 3.5 mm to the sound desk Sound desk videos and music You need: a laptop with an HDMI socket, an HDMI cable long enough to reach, and the projector or TV. In PraiseAV: nothing to set up. The projector gets the congregation view by itself. Good for: small churches, house churches, halls, a first try.","summary":""},{"url":"example-setups.html#2-add-a-screen-for-the-preacher","page":"Example setups","heading":"2. Add a screen for the preacher","text":"2. Add a screen for the preacher The preacher sees their notes, the next slide, the clock and the timer on a TV at the back of the room. Laptop PraiseAV controls HDMI Projector Congregation USB-C to HDMI adaptor, long HDMI cable TV on the back wall Preacher Wi-Fi Preacher’s phone or laptop preacher remote (optional) You need: a laptop with two video outputs (usually HDMI and USB-C), a USB-C to HDMI adaptor, a TV (40 to 55 inch), and a long HDMI cable or an extender (see Hardware ). In PraiseAV: the second screen gets Preacher by itself. Check in Screens… . Good for: preachers who use notes, worship leaders who want the words without turning round.","summary":""},{"url":"example-setups.html#3-two-front-screens-a-back-wall-tv-and-a-timer","page":"Example setups","heading":"3. Two front screens, a back wall TV, and a timer","text":"3. Two front screens, a back wall TV, and a timer A larger room with screens on both sides of the platform. Desktop PC PraiseAV controls HDMI HDMI splitter HDMI splitter HDMI Left projector Congregation HDMI Right projector Congregation Desktop PC PraiseAV controls HDMI over Cat6 extender Back wall TV Preacher HDMI Small screen on the platform floor Timer The two front projectors show the same thing, so a splitter feeds both from one output. Windows sees them as one screen. You need: a desktop PC with a graphics card with three or four outputs , an HDMI splitter (1 in, 2 out), and extenders for the long runs. In PraiseAV: in Screens… , give each output its job: Congregation, Preacher, Timer. Careful: Both projectors on a splitter must handle the same resolution. Set them both to 1920 × 1080.","summary":""},{"url":"example-setups.html#4-livestreaming-with-an-atem-mini","page":"Example setups","heading":"4. Livestreaming with an ATEM Mini","text":"4. Livestreaming with an ATEM Mini Viewers at home see the camera with the song words along the bottom. The ATEM Mini Pro streams straight to YouTube or Facebook, without a second computer. PraiseAV computer HDMI Projector Congregation HDMI ATEM Mini Pro input 2: Livestream screen Camera 1 wide shot from the back HDMI ATEM Mini Pro input 1 Camera 2 the preacher (optional) HDMI ATEM Mini Pro input 3 Sound desk aux send, 3.5 mm ATEM Mini Pro mic input ATEM Mini Pro Ethernet Router internet to YouTube PraiseAV computer Ethernet or Wi-Fi to the same router Router You need: an ATEM Mini Pro, one or two cameras with HDMI out, HDMI cables, an Ethernet cable from the ATEM to the router, and an audio cable from the sound desk. In PraiseAV: In Screens… , give the ATEM output the Livestream job, with a Black background. Click ATEM… , connect, choose Downstream key 1 , and tick Turn the words on and off by themselves . On Sunday, press ● Go live at the top of PraiseAV. Full steps: Livestream and ATEM . Important: The stream needs its own sound mix from the sound desk (an aux send). The sound in the room is mostly from the speakers, so a room microphone sounds hollow and echoey online. Ask whoever runs sound to set up an aux with every microphone and instrument.","summary":""},{"url":"example-setups.html#5-livestreaming-on-a-budget-with-obs","page":"Example setups","heading":"5. Livestreaming on a budget with OBS","text":"5. Livestreaming on a budget with OBS No ATEM: the free OBS Studio does the mixing on the same computer. Camera HDMI USB capture device USB capture device USB 3 PraiseAV computer runs OBS too Sound desk USB audio interface PraiseAV computer runs OBS too PraiseAV computer runs OBS too HDMI Projector Congregation internet YouTube or Facebook In PraiseAV: Screens… › Open a view in a window › Livestream , with a Green background. In OBS, add a Window capture of that window with a Chroma Key filter, above the camera. You need: a more powerful computer (see Hardware ), a capture device, and an audio interface. Good for: trying a stream before buying an ATEM. A webcam works to start with.","summary":""},{"url":"example-setups.html#6-a-camera-on-the-big-screen-and-an-overflow-room","page":"Example setups","heading":"6. A camera on the big screen, and an overflow room","text":"6. A camera on the big screen, and an overflow room In a large room, a camera on the preacher shown on the screens helps people at the back. An overflow hall or café gets the same picture on a TV. Camera on the preacher HDMI USB capture device USB capture device USB 3 PraiseAV computer Live video PraiseAV computer Live video HDMI Projector Congregation: camera with words HDMI splitter, then HDBaseT extender TV in the overflow room same as Congregation In PraiseAV: open Live video , choose Capture device , and Words in a strip along the bottom . See Live video . The overflow room needs the service’s sound too: a feed from the sound desk to a speaker there.","summary":""},{"url":"example-setups.html#7-cameras-from-donated-phones-and-a-recording","page":"Example setups","heading":"7. Cameras from donated phones, and a recording","text":"7. Cameras from donated phones, and a recording No money for cameras or a livestream yet? A couple of old phones and the PraiseAV laptop. Old phone on a shelf at the back Wi-Fi PraiseAV computer Phone camera Old phone by the pulpit Wi-Fi PraiseAV computer Phone camera Sound desk USB audio interface PraiseAV computer recording sound PraiseAV computer Phone camera HDMI Projector camera with the words In PraiseAV: add each phone with Live video › Phone camera › + Add a phone camera , then switch between them as needed. Press ● Record to save the service to a video file, and upload it to YouTube or Facebook afterwards. See Live video and Recording the service . You need: old phones (any from the last few years), phone chargers and long cables, small phone tripods or clamps, and good Wi-Fi in the room.","summary":""},{"url":"example-setups.html#8-setting-up-at-home","page":"Example setups","heading":"8. Setting up at home","text":"8. Setting up at home The AV volunteer can set everything up at home, with no church equipment. Laptop PraiseAV with practice screens HDMI Spare TV or monitor pretend projector In Screens… , click Open practice screens to see every view side by side. Make the themes, alert groups and logo, and plan which screen does what. Save a Church setup to OneDrive or a USB stick, and open it on the church computer. See Church setup . This is also how to show a church what PraiseAV can do before they’ve bought any screens.","summary":""},{"url":"example-setups.html#where-to-put-the-computer","page":"Example setups","heading":"Where to put the computer","text":"Where to put the computer At the back , where the operator can see the screens and the front, and hear clearly. With a clicker or the phone remote , the operator can sit anywhere. Keep cables taped down or under mats where people walk. Plug the computer into the same power as the projector if you can, or use a small UPS (battery backup) so a power blip doesn’t stop the service.","summary":""},{"url":"hardware.html","page":"Hardware: what to buy","heading":"","text":"Hardware: what to buy You don’t need expensive equipment. Most churches can start with a laptop they already have. This page helps when you’re buying, or when something isn’t working well. Note: Prices are rough guides (UK, 2026) and change often. We don’t sell anything or earn from any of these: the makes named are examples that work well, not the only choices.","summary":"Computers, video outputs, projectors and TVs, cables and HDMI extenders for long runs, clickers, cameras, capture cards and which ATEM Mini to choose."},{"url":"hardware.html#the-computer","page":"Hardware: what to buy","heading":"The computer","text":"The computer PraiseAV runs on Windows 10 or 11 . How much computer you need depends on how many screens and cameras you run. An older laptop is fine for the words, but cameras, a livestream and a recording all at once need a newer one.","summary":""},{"url":"hardware.html#which-computer-for-your-setup","page":"Hardware: what to buy","heading":"Which computer for your setup","text":"Which computer for your setup Your setup The computer, roughly Words, the Bible, pictures and videos on one or two screens Most laptops from about 2016 on: Intel Core i3 / AMD Ryzen 3, 8 GB of memory, an SSD Add a preacher screen, one or two cameras, and ◉ Go live or ● Record Intel Core i5 or i7 / AMD Ryzen 5 or 7 from about 2020 on, 16 GB of memory, an SSD with room for recordings, two video outputs Three or more screens showing different things A desktop PC with a graphics card (three or four outputs), or a mini PC with three Three or more cameras An ATEM Mini to mix the cameras and do the streaming, while PraiseAV runs the screens and puts the words over the cameras Each camera plugged in by USB needs its own USB 3 socket (see Capture cards ), and several phone cameras need a good Wi-Fi. These are rough guides, not promises: every computer is different. Try your whole Sunday at home first with practice screens : every screen, the cameras and a test recording, for as long as a service. If the video stutters or the fan roars, the computer is working too hard. Careful: A ten-year-old laptop runs the words happily, but not six cameras, four screens and a livestream at once. Past two or three cameras, let an ATEM Mini do the camera work: then the PraiseAV computer only needs the first row of the table, plus a video output for each screen.","summary":""},{"url":"hardware.html#check-the-computer-you-have","page":"Hardware: what to buy","heading":"Check the computer you have","text":"Check the computer you have Processor and memory : in Windows, open Settings › System › About . Processor shows the model and Installed RAM the memory. Intel: the number after the dash starts with the generation. An i5-8250U is 8th generation (about 2018); an i5-1135G7 is 11th (about 2021). 6th generation or newer is fine for the first row of the table, 10th or newer for the second. AMD: a Ryzen 5 3500U is the 3000 series (about 2019). The 4000 series or newer is fine for the second row. Storage : in Task Manager › Performance , the disk says SSD or HDD . An HDD makes everything slow. Video outputs : count the HDMI and DisplayPort sockets, and the USB-C sockets with a small screen or lightning symbol next to them (see below). USB 3 sockets for cameras: usually blue inside, or marked SS .","summary":""},{"url":"hardware.html#how-many-screens-can-it-drive","page":"Hardware: what to buy","heading":"How many screens can it drive?","text":"How many screens can it drive? Count the video outputs. Each screen showing something different needs its own. Laptops usually have one HDMI socket, and often a USB-C or Thunderbolt socket that can also send video (look for a small screen or lightning symbol next to it). With a USB-C to HDMI adaptor (about £15) that’s two screens, plus the laptop’s own. Desktop PCs with a graphics card usually have three or four outputs (HDMI and DisplayPort). DisplayPort to HDMI adaptors are cheap. Mini PCs (the size of a sandwich) often have two or three outputs, and are good for a permanent install. Careful: “USB display adaptors” (DisplayLink) that plug into an ordinary USB socket work for still slides, but video can stutter. Use real video outputs for the congregation screen.","summary":""},{"url":"hardware.html#before-sunday","page":"Hardware: what to buy","heading":"Before Sunday","text":"Before Sunday Turn off automatic Windows updates restarting during service times ( Settings › Windows Update › Advanced options › Active hours ). Set the power plan so the screen never goes to sleep while plugged in. Turn off notifications ( Focus , or Do not disturb ).","summary":""},{"url":"hardware.html#projectors","page":"Hardware: what to buy","heading":"Projectors","text":"Projectors Room Brightness Small room, lights can be dimmed 3,000 to 3,500 lumens Normal church with windows 4,000 to 5,000 lumens Large or very bright room 6,000 lumens and up Resolution: 1920 × 1080 (“Full HD” or “1080p”) or 1920 × 1200 (“WUXGA”). Avoid “supports 1080p” projectors that are really lower resolution inside. Look for native 1080p. Laser projectors cost more, but last about 20,000 hours with no lamps to replace and switch on instantly. Lamp projectors need a new lamp (£80 to £200) every 3,000 to 5,000 hours. Short-throw projectors sit close to the screen, so people don’t walk through the beam or cast shadows. Makes that churches use a lot: Epson , BenQ , Optoma , Panasonic .","summary":""},{"url":"hardware.html#how-big-a-screen","page":"Hardware: what to buy","heading":"How big a screen?","text":"How big a screen? A useful rule: the screen height should be at least the distance to the back row ÷ 6 . A back row 12 metres away needs a picture at least 2 metres high. For a 16:9 picture, that’s about 3.5 metres wide.","summary":""},{"url":"hardware.html#tvs-instead-of-projectors","page":"Hardware: what to buy","heading":"TVs instead of projectors","text":"TVs instead of projectors Big TVs are now cheaper than projectors for smaller rooms, and work with the lights on. 55 to 75 inch 4K TVs are fine; PraiseAV sends a sharp 1080p picture that looks good on them. On the TV, choose Game or PC mode, and turn off motion smoothing . If the edges of the picture are cut off, find Picture size and choose Just scan , Screen fit or 1:1 . For a preacher’s screen on the back wall, a 40 to 55 inch TV is plenty.","summary":""},{"url":"hardware.html#getting-the-picture-there","page":"Hardware: what to buy","heading":"Getting the picture there","text":"Getting the picture there HDMI cables are reliable up to about 10 to 15 metres . Further than that, the picture flickers or doesn’t appear. Distance Use Up to 10 m An ordinary HDMI cable (“High Speed” or “Premium High Speed”) 10 to 50 m An active optical HDMI cable (fibre inside). One end is marked Source : that end goes to the computer. Up to 70–100 m, or through walls HDMI over Cat6 extenders (look for HDBaseT ). A box at each end, joined by a network cable. Careful: Avoid wireless HDMI and cheap “HDMI over Ethernet” boxes that compress the picture. They drop out or lag, usually mid-service. HDMI splitter (1 in, 2 or 4 out, about £20 to £40): the same picture to several screens, for example two front projectors. Label both ends of every cable (“Front projector”, “Back TV”). It saves a lot of time.","summary":""},{"url":"hardware.html#midi-pedals-and-stream-deck","page":"Hardware: what to buy","heading":"MIDI pedals and Stream Deck","text":"MIDI pedals and Stream Deck A MIDI foot pedal or a keyboard’s sustain pedal lets a worship leader move the words while playing. Any USB MIDI device works. A Stream Deck (Elgato, about £80 to £150) gives the AV desk big labelled buttons. See MIDI and Stream Deck .","summary":""},{"url":"hardware.html#clickers","page":"Hardware: what to buy","heading":"Clickers","text":"Clickers Any presentation clicker works: forward is Next, back is Previous, and the blank button is Black. Look for: A range of 20 metres or more. A USB receiver (no pairing). Examples: Logitech R400 or R500s , and many cheap unbranded ones (about £15 to £40). The phone remote also works as a clicker.","summary":""},{"url":"hardware.html#sound-from-the-computer","page":"Hardware: what to buy","heading":"Sound from the computer","text":"Sound from the computer PraiseAV’s video and music sound comes out of the computer: the headphone socket, or HDMI to the projector. The best way to the sound desk is the headphone socket into a DI box (about £25 to £50), then an XLR cable to the desk. A DI box stops hum and buzz. A small USB audio interface (Behringer UCA202, Focusrite Scarlett Solo) gives better sound and a clean connection.","summary":""},{"url":"hardware.html#cameras","page":"Hardware: what to buy","heading":"Cameras","text":"Cameras For a livestream, or a camera on the big screen. Look for: Clean HDMI output (no menus or icons on the HDMI picture). Runs from mains power for 2 hours or more without stopping. Some photo cameras stop after 30 minutes, or overheat. Optical zoom , to film the preacher from the back of the room. Good choices: Camcorders (Sony Handycam, Panasonic HC-V range, about £250 to £500): simple, reliable, good zoom. PTZ cameras (pan-tilt-zoom, about £500 to £1,500): mounted on a wall and moved by remote, with preset positions (pulpit, band, whole room). Webcams are fine for trying out, but can’t zoom in from the back. Old phones , free: PraiseAV can use any phone on the Wi-Fi as a camera, with no app to install. Add a phone tripod or clamp (about £10) and a long charging cable. See Live video .","summary":""},{"url":"hardware.html#capture-cards","page":"Hardware: what to buy","heading":"Capture cards","text":"Capture cards A camera’s HDMI needs a capture device to go into a computer (for Live video , or OBS). Plug it into a USB 3 socket (usually blue), directly, not through a hub. Elgato Cam Link 4K (about £100): widely used and reliable. Cheaper USB HDMI capture sticks (about £15 to £30) work well for 1080p. An ATEM Mini connected by USB also shows up as a webcam.","summary":""},{"url":"hardware.html#which-atem-mini","page":"Hardware: what to buy","heading":"Which ATEM Mini?","text":"Which ATEM Mini? Blackmagic’s ATEM Mini switchers are small desks that mix cameras and slides. PraiseAV controls all of them. Model Inputs Streams by itself? Records? About ATEM Mini 4 HDMI No: acts as a webcam for OBS or Zoom No £250 ATEM Mini Pro 4 HDMI Yes , over Ethernet to YouTube or Facebook To a USB drive £400 ATEM Mini Pro ISO 4 HDMI Yes Every input separately, for editing later £600 ATEM Mini Extreme 8 HDMI Yes To USB drives £1,000+ For most churches, the ATEM Mini Pro is the one: plug in the cameras and PraiseAV, and stream without another computer. Plug its Ethernet socket into the church router so PraiseAV can control it. Feed it the sound from the desk (an aux send) through its mic input. Update it with ATEM Setup to version 8.0 or later.","summary":""},{"url":"hardware.html#wi-fi-for-phones","page":"Hardware: what to buy","heading":"Wi-Fi for phones","text":"Wi-Fi for phones The phone remote , phone cameras and words on phones don t use the internet. They need a network : the phones and the PraiseAV computer on the same Wi-Fi . The church s Wi-Fi router is enough. It works for this even when the internet connection is down, or there isn t one. A desktop with no Wi-Fi : plug it into the Wi-Fi router with a network cable . The cable and the router s Wi-Fi are one network, so phones on that Wi-Fi can reach it. Use the staff Wi-Fi . A guest Wi-Fi often keeps devices apart, and then phones can t reach the computer. No Wi-Fi in the building? A cheap Wi-Fi router (about £20–30) does it, with no internet line: plug the PraiseAV computer into it with a cable and join the phones to its Wi-Fi. It s the most dependable way, for laptops too. Mobile hotspot (Windows Settings › Network internet ) may work on a laptop, but Windows often won t start it unless the laptop is already on a network, and a desktop needs a Wi-Fi card for it. Try it at home before relying on it on a Sunday. A phone on Wi-Fi with no internet may quietly switch to mobile data. On Android, tap Stay connected if asked; on an iPhone, turn mobile data off.","summary":""},{"url":"hardware.html#internet-for-livestreaming","page":"Hardware: what to buy","heading":"Internet for livestreaming","text":"Internet for livestreaming An upload speed of at least 10 Mbps for a steady 1080p stream (test it at speedtest.net during the week, then again on a Sunday). Use a cable from the ATEM or streaming computer to the router, not Wi-Fi. If the church Wi-Fi is shared with everyone, ask whoever looks after it to keep the streaming cable separate or give it priority.","summary":""},{"url":"hardware.html#shopping-lists","page":"Hardware: what to buy","heading":"Shopping lists","text":"Shopping lists Starting out (setup 1): your laptop, an HDMI cable of the right length, a clicker. About £30 if you already have a projector or TV. With a preacher’s screen (setup 2): add a USB-C to HDMI adaptor, a 50 inch TV with a wall bracket, and an active optical HDMI cable or HDBaseT extender. About £450 to £700. Livestream (setup 4): add an ATEM Mini Pro, a camcorder with clean HDMI, a tripod, HDMI cables, an Ethernet cable and an audio cable from the desk. About £800 to £1,200. See Example setups for how each one fits together.","summary":""},{"url":"hardware.html#equipment-praiseav-doesn-t-support-yet","page":"Hardware: what to buy","heading":"Equipment PraiseAV doesn’t support yet","text":"Equipment PraiseAV doesn’t support yet PraiseAV works with the equipment on this page, and we want it to work with more. Some of it, like the ATEM Mini switchers, has so far been tested against a simulator rather than the real thing, because the equipment is expensive. If you make or sell equipment churches use (video switchers, cameras, projectors, controllers or sound gear) and would like PraiseAV to support it, lend or send us a unit to test with. We’ll do our best to add support, test it properly on the real equipment, and write a guide here on setting it up. Get in touch through praiseav.com .","summary":""},{"url":"church-setup.html","page":"Church setup","heading":"","text":"Church setup The person who looks after AV can set everything up at home, then take it to the church computer in one file. It’s also how two computers (say, the main one and a spare) are kept the same.","summary":"Set PraiseAV up at home and take it to church in one file: which screen does what, themes, alert groups, logo, livestream and ATEM settings."},{"url":"church-setup.html#what-it-includes","page":"Church setup","heading":"What it includes","text":"What it includes Which screen does what, in order (front projector, back wall TV…). Themes, and which theme songs, Bible verses and sermons use. Congregation alert groups and wording. The church logo, and the corner and print logos if you chose them. What the preacher’s screen shows, and how big. Livestream and ATEM settings, transitions, Bible options, CCLI licence number. It doesn’t include songs, services or media. Those go with services and backups .","summary":""},{"url":"church-setup.html#saving-a-church-setup","page":"Church setup","heading":"Saving a church setup","text":"Saving a church setup Click Screens… , then Church setup… . Type the church’s name. Under Screens at church, left to right , list the screens the church has: for example Congregation (front screen) , then Preacher (back wall) . + Add a screen adds another. Click Save church setup . It’s saved in the church folder if you use one (or else with your services), so the church computer sees it by itself. Tip: At home, open Practice screens to see every screen on your laptop while you set things up. Screens not plugged in yet show as Planned on the screen map .","summary":""},{"url":"church-setup.html#the-demo-church","page":"Church setup","heading":"The demo church","text":"The demo church For showing PraiseAV to another church, or practising: in Church setup… , click Load the demo church . In a few seconds PraiseAV is set up as Grace Church , with: a logo (with a print version and a symbol for the corner) the free starter backgrounds , with Soft Light behind the words walk-in music, and congregation alert groups (Crèche, Sunday School, Youth) a whole service: a welcome slide, Amazing Grace (with chords for the band), Psalm 23, a sermon with notes, two more hymns, and notices that go round by themselves Your own setup is saved first under your church’s name in Church setup , so you can open it again afterwards. Songs and saved services aren’t changed. Everything in the demo is free to use: the hymns are public domain, the Bible reading is the BSB, and PraiseAV makes the logo, backgrounds and music itself.","summary":""},{"url":"church-setup.html#using-it-on-the-church-computer","page":"Church setup","heading":"Using it on the church computer","text":"Using it on the church computer Click Screens… , then Church setup… . Under Use a church setup , click the church’s setup. Or Open from somewhere else (USB stick)… . PraiseAV asks first, because it replaces this computer’s themes, alert groups, logo and screen jobs. Songs, services and media aren’t changed. When the screens are plugged in, each gets its job in the order you listed. You can still change any of them in Screens… .","summary":""},{"url":"backups.html","page":"Backups and moving to a new computer","heading":"","text":"Backups and moving to a new computer","summary":"Back up all your songs, settings and services to one file, automatic weekly backups, and moving PraiseAV to a new computer."},{"url":"backups.html#backing-up","page":"Backups and moving to a new computer","heading":"Backing up","text":"Backing up Click Service ▾ (at the top of the Service column), Open , then Backup and moving… . Tick Include media if you want your pictures, videos and music in it too. It shows how big they are. Click Back up now… and choose where. A USB stick or OneDrive is best: somewhere that isn’t this computer. The backup is one file ( .praiseav-backup ) with all your songs, settings (themes, alert groups, logo…), CCLI song usage and saved services.","summary":""},{"url":"backups.html#automatic-backups","page":"Backups and moving to a new computer","heading":"Automatic backups","text":"Automatic backups Once a week, PraiseAV backs up your songs, settings and services by itself (not media) to Documents › PraiseAV Backups . It keeps the last 8. The Backup window says when the last one was. Tip: If your Documents folder is in OneDrive (it often is on new computers), these backups are off the computer already.","summary":""},{"url":"backups.html#moving-to-a-new-computer","page":"Backups and moving to a new computer","heading":"Moving to a new computer","text":"Moving to a new computer On the old computer, back up with media . Install PraiseAV on the new computer. Click Service ▾ , Open , Backup and moving… , then Choose a backup… . It says what’s in it: which computer, when, how many songs and services. Click Bring it in .","summary":""},{"url":"backups.html#bringing-in-a-backup-is-safe","page":"Backups and moving to a new computer","heading":"Bringing in a backup is safe","text":"Bringing in a backup is safe Nothing on this computer is deleted. Songs you already have are skipped, and files already here are kept. So you can bring in a backup from another computer to add its songs without losing your own. The church’s settings (themes, logo…) are taken from the backup.","summary":""},{"url":"backups.html#where-praiseav-keeps-things","page":"Backups and moving to a new computer","heading":"Where PraiseAV keeps things","text":"Where PraiseAV keeps things What Where Media Documents › PraiseAV Media (or the folder you chose) Automatic backups Documents › PraiseAV Backups Services Wherever you chose in Open (this computer, OneDrive, Google Drive…) Songs and settings Inside PraiseAV. Use a backup to copy them. Shared songs The PraiseAV Songs folder you chose in Share songs… (see Songs )","summary":""},{"url":"troubleshooting.html","page":"Troubleshooting","heading":"","text":"Troubleshooting Important: In the middle of a service, press Black ( B ). It always works, and gives you a moment to sort things out.","summary":"What to do when the projector shows nothing, the wrong screen shows the words, a video won't play, there's no sound, PraiseAV closed mid-service, or a phone or ATEM won't connect."},{"url":"troubleshooting.html#the-projector-shows-nothing","page":"Troubleshooting","heading":"The projector shows nothing","text":"The projector shows nothing Is Black or Logo on at the top? Press it again. Is the projector on, and on the right input (HDMI 1, HDMI 2…)? Use its remote. Press Windows + P and choose Extend . In PraiseAV, click ⋯ More › Hide the screens at the top right, then Show the screens . Open Screens… . Is the projector listed, with the Congregation job? Unplug the HDMI cable at both ends and plug it back in.","summary":""},{"url":"troubleshooting.html#the-projector-shows-my-desktop-or-a-copy-of-my-screen","page":"Troubleshooting","heading":"The projector shows my desktop, or a copy of my screen","text":"The projector shows my desktop, or a copy of my screen Windows is copying (duplicating) your screen onto the projector. PraiseAV notices this and shows a yellow message at the top: click Use it as a separate screen . Or press Windows + P and choose Extend . PraiseAV then finds the projector and puts the words on it.","summary":""},{"url":"troubleshooting.html#the-words-are-on-the-wrong-screen","page":"Troubleshooting","heading":"The words are on the wrong screen","text":"The words are on the wrong screen Open Screens… . On the screen map , tap the two screens to swap them. PraiseAV remembers this for next time.","summary":""},{"url":"troubleshooting.html#a-video-won-t-play","page":"Troubleshooting","heading":"A video won’t play","text":"A video won’t play PraiseAV says if a file won’t play. Some formats (WMV, AVI, ProRes) can’t be read. Convert the video to MP4 with the free HandBrake app (choose the “Fast 1080p30” preset) and add it again. If a video stutters, it may be 4K or very large. A 1080p MP4 plays smoothly on almost any computer.","summary":""},{"url":"troubleshooting.html#no-sound","page":"Troubleshooting","heading":"No sound","text":"No sound Click the speaker icon by the clock (bottom right of Windows) and check the right output is chosen and not muted. Check the Volume under Music and audio. Check the sound desk channel is up. Videos play their sound from the congregation screen’s window; music from Music and audio. Both use the Windows output.","summary":""},{"url":"troubleshooting.html#the-words-are-too-small-or-too-big","page":"Troubleshooting","heading":"The words are too small or too big","text":"The words are too small or too big Go to the Themes tab, click the theme, and change Largest text size . See Themes . Long verses shrink by themselves to fit.","summary":""},{"url":"troubleshooting.html#a-powerpoint-won-t-come-in","page":"Troubleshooting","heading":"A PowerPoint won’t come in","text":"A PowerPoint won’t come in PraiseAV needs Microsoft PowerPoint or the free LibreOffice to import PowerPoint files automatically. Install LibreOffice, or ask for a PDF. If you use ONLYOFFICE Desktop Editors or Apache OpenOffice , open the presentation there, export it as a PDF, then add that PDF to PraiseAV. Check the exported slides before the service: animations and embedded videos will not play in the PDF. See PowerPoint and PDF .","summary":""},{"url":"troubleshooting.html#a-powerpoint-s-points-all-show-at-once-or-its-video-doesn-t-play","page":"Troubleshooting","heading":"A PowerPoint’s points all show at once, or its video doesn’t play","text":"A PowerPoint’s points all show at once, or its video doesn’t play Live animations and videos need supported Microsoft PowerPoint or LibreOffice Impress on the service computer. PraiseAV tries PowerPoint first, then Impress. If it says Shown as pictures , neither could play the file: open it in the installed program once to check for a password or import warning, then test it again. Impress can render PowerPoint effects differently, so check the actual slide show. See PowerPoint and PDF .","summary":""},{"url":"troubleshooting.html#the-camera-isn-t-in-the-list","page":"Troubleshooting","heading":"The camera isn’t in the list","text":"The camera isn’t in the list Click Refresh in Live video. Close any other program using the camera (Zoom, Teams, OBS, the Camera app). Plug the capture device into a blue USB 3 socket, directly, not through a hub. See Live video .","summary":""},{"url":"troubleshooting.html#a-phone-can-t-connect-to-the-remote","page":"Troubleshooting","heading":"A phone can’t connect to the remote","text":"A phone can’t connect to the remote It must be on the same Wi-Fi as the PraiseAV computer (not guest Wi-Fi), and Windows must allow PraiseAV on private networks. See Phone remote .","summary":""},{"url":"troubleshooting.html#obs-or-the-other-room-can-t-show-the-words","page":"Troubleshooting","heading":"OBS or the other room can’t show the words","text":"OBS or the other room can’t show the words The other computer or TV must be on the same Wi-Fi as the PraiseAV computer, and Windows must allow PraiseAV on private networks. OBS on the PraiseAV computer itself can use the on this computer address. See Screens on the network .","summary":""},{"url":"troubleshooting.html#a-phone-camera-won-t-connect","page":"Troubleshooting","heading":"A phone camera won’t connect","text":"A phone camera won’t connect The phone must be on the same Wi-Fi as the PraiseAV computer (not guest Wi-Fi). If the phone says the connection isn’t private, tap Advanced (or Show details ) and Continue . That’s expected. If it says the camera wasn’t allowed, allow the camera for the page in the browser’s settings. If the computer’s Wi-Fi address changed, scan the new code in Live video › Phone camera › + Add a phone camera . See Live video .","summary":""},{"url":"troubleshooting.html#a-recording-has-no-sound","page":"Troubleshooting","heading":"A recording has no sound","text":"A recording has no sound In ● Record , check Sound from is the sound desk’s input, and that the desk is sending sound to it. This computer’s sound only records PraiseAV’s own videos and music. See Recording the service .","summary":""},{"url":"troubleshooting.html#praiseav-says-live-but-youtube-says-quot-no-data-quot","page":"Troubleshooting","heading":"PraiseAV says LIVE, but YouTube says No data","text":"PraiseAV says LIVE, but YouTube says No data PraiseAV is sending, but YouTube can t match it to the broadcast you re watching. In YouTube Studio, open Stream health (under the preview): it says which part is getting the stream. Vertical view: Stream is healthy but Horizontal view: No data : PraiseAV has the vertical stream key. YouTube s Dual stream shows two keys: the main one in the left panel (for example Key: Test , or Default stream key ) and a Vertical one on the right for the Shorts feed. In YouTube Studio, click the copy button beside the key in the left panel. Then in PraiseAV: ◉ Go live › Change , paste, Save , and go live again. (You can leave Dual stream on: PraiseAV works with it.) No data everywhere : check the key in PraiseAV ends with the same four characters as the one in YouTube Studio ( ◉ Go live shows them), that you re looking at the right broadcast ( Manage lists them), and that live streaming has been switched on for the channel (the first time can take a day). Preparing stream for more than a minute (while Stream health says Excellent): update PraiseAV. Older versions sent a picture of an odd shape, which YouTube s Dual stream couldn t use. If you can t update yet, switch Dual stream off in Stream settings , then in PraiseAV stop the livestream and ● Go live now again. The preview stays black or frozen : refresh the YouTube Studio page (F5). YouTube s preview can lag behind; Stream health is the one to trust.","summary":""},{"url":"troubleshooting.html#the-atem-won-t-connect","page":"Troubleshooting","heading":"The ATEM won’t connect","text":"The ATEM won’t connect The ATEM must be plugged into the network with an Ethernet cable , the same network as the computer. Check its address in Blackmagic’s ATEM Setup app (with the ATEM plugged in by USB). A new ATEM Mini uses 192.168.10.240 . If your router gives addresses like 192.168.1.x , change the ATEM’s address in ATEM Setup to one in that range (for example 192.168.1.240 ). See Livestream and ATEM .","summary":""},{"url":"troubleshooting.html#the-laptop-went-to-sleep-or-the-projector-went-blank","page":"Troubleshooting","heading":"The laptop went to sleep, or the projector went blank","text":"The laptop went to sleep, or the projector went blank While PraiseAV is showing the screens, or the phone remote is on, it keeps the laptop awake. If it still sleeps: Are PraiseAV’s screens hidden? Click Show the screens in the top bar. Check the laptop is plugged in: with a very low battery, Windows may sleep anyway. PraiseAV shows the battery at the top when it’s unplugged. Closing the laptop’s lid usually sends it to sleep. Leave it open, or in Windows Control Panel › Power Options › Choose what closing the lid does , choose Do nothing when plugged in. ✓ Ready for Sunday? warns you about the lid, and about Windows’ sleep timer while the screens are hidden.","summary":""},{"url":"troubleshooting.html#the-power-went-off-or-praiseav-closed","page":"Troubleshooting","heading":"The power went off, or PraiseAV closed","text":"The power went off, or PraiseAV closed Open PraiseAV again. Your current service is still there: it’s kept as you work, all the time. If it closed in the middle of a service, PraiseAV says PraiseAV closed unexpectedly : click Carry on where I was and the same slide comes back on the screens, as it was. See Running a service .","summary":""},{"url":"troubleshooting.html#before-the-service-is-everything-ready","page":"Troubleshooting","heading":"Before the service: is everything ready?","text":"Before the service: is everything ready? Click ✓ Ready for Sunday? at the top of the Service column. It finds most of the problems on this page before they happen: a projector that isn’t plugged in or is showing a copy of the laptop, a missing video or picture, no sound output, no internet for live video, a laptop on its battery, and Windows waiting to restart for an update. See Running a service .","summary":""},{"url":"troubleshooting.html#windows-restarted-for-an-update-during-the-service","page":"Troubleshooting","heading":"Windows restarted for an update during the service","text":"Windows restarted for an update during the service Windows restarts for updates outside its active hours . Open PraiseAV again and click Carry on where I was . To stop it happening again, in Windows Settings › Windows Update › Advanced options › Active hours , set the hours to cover your services, or click Pause updates before a service. ✓ Ready for Sunday? warns you if an update is waiting to restart.","summary":""},{"url":"troubleshooting.html#a-song-or-service-is-missing","page":"Troubleshooting","heading":"A song or service is missing","text":"A song or service is missing Saved services: click Open , then Church folder… : is this computer using the same church folder as the other one? Songs: search in the Songs tab by a line of the words, not just the title. Bring them back from a backup: Open › Backup and moving… › Choose a backup… . Nothing you have is deleted. See Backups .","summary":""},{"url":"troubleshooting.html#still-stuck","page":"Troubleshooting","heading":"Still stuck?","text":"Still stuck? Click Report a problem… at the bottom right of PraiseAV. Say what you were doing, what you expected and what happened, then click Email it… : an email opens, and the report is saved in Documents › PraiseAV reports , ready to attach. A photo of the screen helps too. The report adds facts that help find the problem: the Windows and PraiseAV versions, the screens and what each one does, which features are on, recent errors, and the ATEM and livestream logs. It never includes songs, services, names or the stream key. Click See everything in the report to read every word before you send it. Nothing is sent until you send the email yourself. No email on this computer? Click Save as a file or Copy , and send it another way, or ask through praiseav.com .","summary":""}];
